window.__hvBdRegister("livy-3", {"key":"livy-3","sections":[{"i":0,"j":8,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 1–5","title":"Preface, the Barcids, and Hannibal's rise","summary":"Livy opens by calling the war with Hannibal the most memorable ever waged, fought between peoples at the height of their power and with great bitterness. He tells how the nine-year-old Hannibal swore at the altar, at Hamilcar's bidding, to be Rome's enemy, and traces the command in Spain from Hamilcar to his son-in-law Hasdrubal, who made the Ebro treaty with Rome. When Hasdrubal is assassinated, the army hails Hannibal as general; Livy recalls Hanno's earlier objection in the Carthaginian senate to sending him to Spain, and sketches Hannibal's tireless virtues and monstrous vices. Hannibal then subdues the Olcades, Vaccaei and Carpetani, routing a vast host at the Tagus, until everything south of the Ebro except Saguntum is Carthaginian.","ideas":["The war as the greatest and most evenly matched of all wars","Hannibal's boyhood oath of enmity to Rome","The Ebro treaty and the guaranteed independence of Saguntum","Hannibal's character: endurance and courage joined to cruelty and perfidy"],"terms":[{"t":"Barcine faction","d":"The party at Carthage attached to Hamilcar Barca's family, strong with the soldiers and commons and opposed by Hanno."},{"t":"praetorium","d":"The general's headquarters in camp, into which the soldiers carried the young Hannibal to acclaim him."}]},{"i":9,"j":23,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 6–15","title":"The siege and fall of Saguntum","summary":"Saguntum appeals to Rome as Hannibal stirs up its neighbours, and the senate sends envoys; meanwhile Hannibal besieges the city, is wounded in the thigh, and breaches its walls, while the defenders resist fiercely with the fiery phalarica. Hannibal refuses to receive the Roman envoys, who go on to Carthage, where Hanno alone urges the senate to surrender Hannibal and keep the treaty; the senate sides with Hannibal. The siege resumes under Hannibal and Maharbal; Alorcus brings harsh terms to the Saguntine senate, whereupon the leading men burn the city's gold and silver and themselves, a tower falls, and Hannibal takes the city and puts the adults to the sword. Livy closes by noting a chronological difficulty about the siege's length and the consuls of the year.","ideas":["Saguntine loyalty to allies even to their own ruin","Hanno's warning that the war with Saguntum will become a war with Rome","Alorcus's terms: leave the city with two garments each","Mass self-immolation and the sack of the city"],"terms":[{"t":"phalarica","d":"A Saguntine javelin with a long iron head wrapped in pitch-smeared tow, set alight and hurled to force soldiers to drop their shields."},{"t":"pent-houses (vineae)","d":"Movable sheds pushed up to the walls to cover besiegers and their battering-rams."}]},{"i":24,"j":36,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 16–23","title":"War declared; Hannibal sets out from Spain","summary":"Rome, stunned by Saguntum's fall, assigns Spain to the consul Cornelius and Africa with Sicily to Sempronius, raises legions and fleets, and has the people vote for war. An embassy led by Quintus Fabius asks Carthage whether Hannibal acted with public sanction; a Carthaginian argues about treaties, and Fabius offers war or peace from the fold of his toga, and the Carthaginians accept war. Livy defends Rome's treaty position; the envoys then find the Spaniards, warned by Saguntum's fate, and the armed Gauls hostile or scornful, with only Massilia friendly. Hannibal furloughs his Spaniards, pays vows at Gades, sends Spanish troops to Africa and leaves Hasdrubal in Spain, dreams of a divine guide and a serpent signifying Italy's devastation, and crosses the Ebro and Pyrenees, releasing troops who balk at the march.","ideas":["Fabius's choice of war or peace from the toga's fold","The Carthaginian argument that Hasdrubal's treaty did not bind the state","Spanish and Gallic refusals to side with Rome","Hannibal's dream of the guide and the serpent"],"terms":[{"t":"supplication (supplicatio)","d":"A public prayer held throughout the city, here to ask the gods for a good outcome of the war decreed by the people."},{"t":"Libyphoenicians","d":"A people of mixed Punic and African blood who supplied cavalry to Hasdrubal's army in Spain."}]},{"i":37,"j":49,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 24–31","title":"Through Gaul and across the Rhone","summary":"Hannibal buys the passage of the Gauls at Ruscino with gifts. In Italy the Boi and Insubres revolt over the new colonies at Placentia and Cremona, trap Roman commissioners at Mutina, and ambush the praetor Manlius in the woods; Atilius is sent to relieve him. Cornelius lands at the Rhone mouth while Hannibal, opposed by the Volcae, sends Hanno upstream to cross and take them in the rear, then ferries his army over; Livy describes the rafting of the elephants. A Roman and a Numidian cavalry patrol clash, Boian envoys urge Hannibal toward Italy, and he rallies his frightened troops by belittling the Alps, then marches up the Rhone, settles an Allobrogian succession dispute, and crosses the flooded Druentia.","ideas":["Boian revolt and Manlius's ambush in the forest","The Rhone crossing with a flanking force under Hanno","Elephants carried over on earth-covered rafts","Hannibal's speech: the Alps are only high mountains"],"terms":[{"t":"the Island","d":"The name for the plain enclosed where the Isara and Rhone meet, near the Allobroges."}]},{"i":50,"j":59,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 32–38","title":"The passage of the Alps","summary":"Cornelius, finding Hannibal gone from the Rhone, sends his brother Gnaeus with most of the army to Spain and sails back to meet Hannibal in Italy. Hannibal's army is dismayed by the Alps; he outwits mountaineers who hold a pass only by day, survives a chaotic attack in the defile, and is nearly destroyed by a treacherous tribe that offers hostages and guides and then ambushes the column. On the ninth day they reach the summit, where Hannibal shows his men Italy and the Po plains; the steep descent, fresh snow and ice, and a landslip force four days of road-building, the rock split with fire and vinegar. Livy reckons the march at five months from New Carthage and fifteen days over the Alps, weighs disputed troop numbers, and rejects the Poenine and Cremo routes.","ideas":["Night seizure of the heights abandoned by the mountaineers","Treacherous peace offer followed by ambush","Hannibal points out Italy from the summit","Cutting a road through rock with fire and vinegar"],"terms":[{"t":"Poenine Pass","d":"A route Livy rejects for Hannibal's crossing, noting its name comes from the mountain god Poeninus, not the Poeni."}]},{"i":60,"j":70,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 39–45","title":"Before the Ticinus: the generals' speeches","summary":"Hannibal's worn army recovers while he takes the Taurine capital; Scipio hurries to the Po with raw troops, and each general has formed an admiration for the other. Scipio tells his soldiers the Carthaginians are a beaten people and mere shadows after the Alps, recalls Rome's mercy after the First Punic War, and says they must stand as if before the walls of Rome. Hannibal has captive mountaineers fight in pairs for freedom, then tells his men that, hemmed in by seas, the Po and the Alps, they must conquer or die, and that Rome's riches will be their reward. As the Romans bridge the Ticinus, Hannibal promises land, money and citizenship and seals the pledge by killing a lamb with a flint.","ideas":["Mutual respect between Scipio and Hannibal","Scipio: fight the Carthaginians like rebellious slaves","The captives' combat as a picture of the army's own plight","Hannibal: contempt of death is the sharpest weapon"],"terms":[{"t":"Aegatian islands and Eryx","d":"Scenes of Carthage's defeat in the First Punic War, which both speakers invoke."}]},{"i":71,"j":80,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 46–52","title":"The Ticinus and the war off Sicily","summary":"After ill omens, Scipio's reconnaissance meets Hannibal's cavalry; the Numidians envelop the Romans, and the wounded consul is saved by his son, the future Africanus (Coelius credits a Ligurian slave). Scipio withdraws across the Po to Placentia, Hannibal follows, Gallic auxiliaries desert from the Roman camp, and Scipio retires to the Trebia, while Hannibal buys Clastidium's grain store from its commander. Off Sicily Hiero captures Carthaginian ships, the praetor Aemilius defeats a Punic fleet near Lilybaeum, and Sempronius takes Melita before the senate recalls him to join his colleague on the Trebia. The two consuls now disagree: Cornelius wants delay, Sempronius battle.","ideas":["Carthaginian superiority in cavalry shown at the Ticinus","Young Scipio rescues his father","Desertion of the Gallic auxiliaries and the bribed surrender of Clastidium","Roman naval success at Lilybaeum and the capture of Melita"],"terms":[{"t":"Africanus","d":"The surname later earned by the consul's son for his final victory over Hannibal."}]},{"i":81,"j":90,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 52–57","title":"The battle of the Trebia","summary":"Hannibal ravages the neutral Gauls' land; Sempronius defends them in a successful skirmish and, elated and eager for glory before the elections, overrides the wounded Cornelius. Hannibal hides Mago with two thousand picked men in an overgrown watercourse and sends Numidians to lure the Romans across the icy, swollen Trebia before breakfast in midwinter. His fed and oiled troops, Balearic slingers and elephants rout the Roman cavalry, Mago strikes from the rear, and about ten thousand Romans break through the centre to Placentia while many others perish in the river and the cold. Rome is terrified; Sempronius holds the elections, Servilius and Flaminius are chosen, and in winter Hannibal is wounded at a fortified depot near Placentia and sacks Victumulae after its surrender.","ideas":["Sempronius's rashness against Cornelius's caution","Mago's ambush in the watercourse","Romans chilled, hungry and soaked against a rested enemy","Breakout of ten thousand to Placentia"],"terms":[{"t":"Baliares","d":"Light-armed slingers from the Balearic Islands, placed before Hannibal's line and then on the wings."},{"t":"Cenomani","d":"The only Gallic tribe that stayed loyal to Rome at the Trebia."}]},{"i":91,"j":100,"loc":"Book XXI, chs. 58–63 and Summary","title":"Apennine storm, Spain, prodigies, and Flaminius","summary":"Hannibal's march into Etruria is halted by a storm on the Apennines that kills men, horses and seven elephants; he turns back and fights Sempronius to a costly draw near Placentia before retiring to Liguria. In Spain Gnaeus Scipio wins the coast north of the Ebro, defeats and captures Hanno near Cissis, and subdues the Ilergetes and Ausetani while Hasdrubal raids and withdraws. At Rome many prodigies are reported and expiated by the Sibylline Books. Flaminius, hated by the senate, slips off to enter his consulship at Ariminum without the customary rites, ignores the senate's recall, and is marked by an ill-omened sacrifice; a closing summary recapitulates the book.","ideas":["The Apennine storm rivalling the Alps","Gnaeus Scipio's victory at Cissis","Prodigies and their expiation","Flaminius's defiance of the senate and the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"lectisternium","d":"A rite in which images of gods were placed on couches and served a feast, ordered here to expiate prodigies."},{"t":"Sibylline Books","d":"Prophetic books consulted by the decemviri to prescribe expiations."}]},{"i":101,"j":109,"loc":"Book XXII 1–2","title":"Prodigies at Rome and the Arno marshes","summary":"As spring comes, Hannibal leaves winter quarters early, partly to escape plots by the restless Gauls, who now resent bearing the burden of the war. At Rome the senate complains that Flaminius left the City without proper auspices, and a long list of prodigies is reported and expiated by sacrifices, a supplication, gifts to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, and a public Saturnalia. Hannibal, learning that Flaminius is at Arretium, takes the shorter route through the flooded Arno marshes. The Gauls suffer worst in four days and three nights without sleep, and Hannibal himself loses the sight of one eye.","ideas":["The Gauls turn their hatred from Rome to Hannibal when their own lands become the seat of war","Flaminius is charged with leaving Rome without the auspices","Prodigies are expiated on the advice of the decemvirs and the Sacred Books","The march through the marshes costs many men and beasts and Hannibal's eye"],"terms":[{"t":"lectisternium","d":"A rite in which images of the gods were set on couches and offered a banquet."},{"t":"decemvirs (sacris faciundis)","d":"The board of ten who consulted the Sibylline Books and directed the rites they prescribed."}]},{"i":110,"j":121,"loc":"Book XXII 3–7","title":"Flaminius trapped at Lake Trasumennus","summary":"Hannibal, judging Flaminius proud and reckless, ravages Etruria before his eyes to provoke him. Flaminius rejects his council's advice to wait for his colleague, and ignores the omens of his stumbling horse and the standard that will not be pulled up. Hannibal lays an ambush in the narrow pass between Lake Trasumennus and the hills, and in the morning mist the Romans are attacked from every side; the fighting is so intense that no one feels an earthquake. After three hours Flaminius is killed by the Insubrian Ducarius, the army breaks, and six thousand who escaped surrender to Maharbal and are put in chains. Livy, following Fabius Pictor, gives fifteen thousand Roman dead.","ideas":["Flaminius's rashness and contempt for the gods are set against his officers' caution","The ambush between the lake and the mountains under cover of mist","Every soldier becomes his own commander once retreat is cut off","Hannibal frees the Latin allies but keeps the Romans as captives"],"terms":[{"t":"Trasumennus","d":"The lake in Etruria where Hannibal ambushed and destroyed Flaminius's army."}]},{"i":122,"j":129,"loc":"Book XXII 7–10","title":"Grief at Rome and Fabius named dictator","summary":"At Rome the praetor Pomponius announces simply that a great battle has been lost, and crowds, women especially, wait at the gates for news of kinsmen; two mothers are said to die of joy. News follows that four thousand cavalry under Centenius have also been lost. With the consul absent, the people for the first time elect a dictator, Quintus Fabius Maximus, with Marcus Minucius Rufus as master of the horse. Fabius blames Flaminius's neglect of ceremonies, and on the advice of the Sibylline Books a Sacred Spring, great games, temples to Venus Erycina and Mens, and a lectisternium are vowed.","ideas":["Livy compares the state to a sick body that feels even small blows","The dictator is created by popular vote, an unprecedented step","Hannibal fails before Spoletium and turns aside into Picenum and Apulia","The formal vow of the Sacred Spring, set out word for word"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"An emergency magistrate with supreme authority for up to six months, assisted by a master of the horse."},{"t":"ver sacrum (Sacred Spring)","d":"A vow to sacrifice to Jupiter all the livestock born in a given spring if the state survived."}]},{"i":130,"j":136,"loc":"Book XXII 11–14","title":"Fabius shadows Hannibal; Minucius chafes","summary":"Fabius takes over Servilius's army, raises two new legions, orders the country people to burn their crops and withdraw, and makes the consul approach him without lictors. Facing Hannibal near Arpi, he refuses battle, keeps to the heights and wins small skirmishes that restore his men's confidence, which Hannibal privately fears. Minucius openly calls Fabius slothful and timid. Hannibal, misled by a guide who confuses Casinum with Casilinum, ravages the Falernian country, and as Fabius watches from Mount Massicus, Minucius delivers a fiery speech demanding battle and invoking Camillus and Papirius Cursor.","ideas":["Fabius's strategy: shadow the enemy, never risk a general engagement","Minucius disparages caution as cowardice, gaining the soldiers' favour","The allies stay loyal despite the devastation, because Roman rule is just","The guide's mistake over Casinum and Casilinum and his crucifixion"],"terms":[{"t":"magister equitum (master of the horse)","d":"The dictator's second-in-command, normally wholly subordinate to him."}]},{"i":137,"j":143,"loc":"Book XXII 15–18","title":"Hannibal escapes with the burning oxen","summary":"Fabius holds to his waiting policy despite its unpopularity and blocks the passes by which Hannibal must leave the Falernian district. His cavalry officer Mancinus disobeys orders and is killed in a fight with Carthalo's horse. Hemmed in near Casilinum, Hannibal ties burning brushwood to the horns of two thousand cattle and drives them up the hills at night; the Roman guard on the pass thinks it is surrounded and flees, and the army slips through to Allifae. Fabius follows along the ridges, then is called to Rome, warning Minucius to trust prudence over fortune.","ideas":["Fabius's trap at Mount Callicula and Casilinum","The ruse of the fire-horned oxen","Spanish light troops beat heavy Roman infantry in rough country","Fabius compares his strategy to a physician's prescription of rest"],"terms":[]},{"i":144,"j":155,"loc":"Book XXII 19–22","title":"Roman successes in Spain","summary":"In Spain Hasdrubal advances by land and sea, but Gnaeus Scipio surprises the Punic fleet at the mouth of the Ebro, capturing twenty-five of forty ships and gaining command of the coast. The Romans raid Onusa, the region of New Carthage, Longuntica and Ebusus, and over a hundred and twenty communities submit. A rising by Mandonius and Indibilis is crushed, and the Celtiberians defeat Hasdrubal twice. Publius Scipio arrives with reinforcements, and the Saguntine Abelux tricks the Carthaginian Bostar into releasing the Spanish hostages, then delivers them to the Romans, who restore them and win the gratitude of Spain.","ideas":["A naval panic hands the Romans the Spanish coast","The Scipio brothers wage the war in harmony","Abelux's double-dealing with the hostages held at Saguntum","Roman clemency at first contact earns more gratitude than Carthage could"],"terms":[]},{"i":156,"j":165,"loc":"Book XXII 23–26","title":"Minucius's success and the equalized command","summary":"Fabius grows unpopular: Hannibal spares his farm to make him look like a traitor, and Fabius sells it to pay a ransom for prisoners that the senate would not fund. In his absence Minucius brings the camp down to the plain and routs Hannibal's foragers near Gereonium, a success inflated at Rome. The tribune Metilius accuses Fabius of prolonging the war to keep power and proposes making the master of the horse equal to the dictator. Fabius argues that a good commander governs fortune by reason, holds the consular election, and leaves for the army; the bill passes, championed only by Gaius Terentius Varro, a butcher's son turned demagogue.","ideas":["Hannibal's ruse of sparing Fabius's estate","Fabius pays the state's debt for prisoners out of his own purse","Metilius's bill divides supreme command, an unheard-of step","Fabius bears the insult with unruffled calm"],"terms":[{"t":"plebiscite","d":"A measure passed by the assembly of the plebs on a tribune's motion."}]},{"i":166,"j":177,"loc":"Book XXII 27–31","title":"Fabius rescues Minucius from Hannibal's trap","summary":"Minucius boasts of his triumph over Fabius and proposes alternating command; Fabius instead divides the army and the two camp apart. Hannibal hides five thousand men in hollows of an apparently bare valley and lures Minucius into a battle for a hill, then springs the ambush. Fabius marches out and saves the routed legions, and Hannibal says he has beaten Minucius but Fabius has beaten him. Minucius tells his men that the next best man is the one who heeds good advice, leads them to Fabius's camp and hails him as Father. Meanwhile Servilius's raid on Africa ends in a bloody rout, and Livy notes that Fabius was strictly an acting dictator.","ideas":["Command divided by army rather than by days","The hidden ambush in a treeless valley","Minucius's public submission and the title of Father","Hannibal's image of the storm cloud breaking from the mountain-tops"],"terms":[{"t":"pro dictatore (acting dictator)","d":"Livy's point that Fabius, elected by the people, was properly an acting dictator, not a nominated one."}]},{"i":178,"j":189,"loc":"Book XXII 32–37","title":"Winter, elections, and Varro's consulship","summary":"The consuls Servilius and Atilius continue Fabius's strategy until Hannibal is near starving. Neapolis offers gold bowls, a spy and conspiring slaves are punished, and envoys go to Philip, the Ligurians and Pineus. After a flawed dictatorship and an interregnum, the tribune Herennius claims the nobles are spinning out the war, and Varro alone is elected consul; the nobles force the reluctant Lucius Aemilius Paulus to stand as his colleague. The armies are enlarged, prodigies are expiated, and King Hiero sends grain, archers, a golden Victory, and advice to strike Africa.","ideas":["Bitter patrician-plebeian struggle over the consulship","Varro is elected as the plebs' new man; Paulus as a check on him","Only experienced men are chosen for office in the crisis","Rome accepts Hiero's golden Victory for its omen but declines allies' gold"],"terms":[{"t":"interrex","d":"A senator who held office for five days to conduct elections when no consuls were available."},{"t":"novus homo (new man)","d":"A man who was the first of his family to reach high office."}]},{"i":190,"j":198,"loc":"Book XXII 38–40","title":"Fabius's counsel to Paulus before Cannae","summary":"For the first time the tribunes formally administer an oath that soldiers will not leave the ranks out of fear. Varro boasts that he will end the war the day he sees the enemy; Paulus replies that plans must follow circumstances. Fabius privately warns Paulus that Varro will be a worse opponent than Hannibal, and argues that Rome, fighting at home among loyal allies, will win by waiting while Hannibal, in hostile country and short of food, weakens daily. Paulus agrees but doubts he can restrain his colleague; at the front the armies are joined, and Hannibal welcomes the new consuls, his supplies nearly exhausted.","ideas":["The voluntary soldiers' pledge becomes a sworn oath","Varro's and Paulus's opposite speeches to the people","Fabius: reason, not fortune, should guide; haste is blind","Hannibal has only ten days' grain left and the Spaniards are ready to desert"],"terms":[{"t":"ius iurandum (military oath)","d":"The oath binding soldiers to their commander, here extended to a pledge not to flee."}]},{"i":199,"j":209,"loc":"Book XXII 41–45","title":"Manoeuvres and quarrels before Cannae","summary":"After a skirmish won by the Romans, Paulus checks the pursuit, angering Varro. Hannibal leaves his camp full of riches as bait, but Statilius reports a trap, and when unfavourable omens halt the army, two escaped slaves reveal the ambush. Short of grain and facing mutiny, Hannibal marches to Apulia and camps by Cannae with the Volturnus wind at his back. The consuls follow and make two camps; the Numidians harass the water-carriers, and on his day of command Varro leads the army across the Aufidus without consulting Paulus and draws it up for battle.","ideas":["Alternating daily command between consuls of opposite temper","The deserted-camp ruse detected by Statilius and the sacred fowls","Hannibal considers abandoning his infantry and fleeing to Gaul","Varro forces battle on his own day of command"],"terms":[{"t":"Volturnus (wind)","d":"A local wind that blew dust into the Romans' faces at Cannae."}]},{"i":210,"j":219,"loc":"Book XXII 46–50","title":"The Battle of Cannae","summary":"Hannibal draws up Gallic and Spanish cavalry by the river, Numidians on the right, and an infantry centre of Gauls and Spaniards projecting forward, with Africans in Roman arms on both ends. The Roman cavalry is crushed in a cramped fight; the legions drive in the Gallic and Spanish wedge, only to be enveloped by the Africans and closed in from behind. Feigned Numidian deserters strike from the rear, and Hasdrubal's cavalry completes the rout. Paulus, wounded, refuses Lentulus's offer of a horse, sends a message to Fabius, and dies among his men, while Varro flees to Venusia; Livy gives forty-five thousand five hundred foot and two thousand seven hundred horse killed.","ideas":["The crescent formation that turns the Roman advance into encirclement","The ruse of the five hundred false deserters","Paulus's dying message to the senate and to Fabius","Livy judges Cannae more grievous than the Allia"],"terms":[{"t":"Cannae","d":"The Apulian village by the Aufidus where Hannibal destroyed the Roman army in 216 BC."}]},{"i":220,"j":228,"loc":"Book XXII 50–54","title":"After Cannae: survivors, Maharbal and Scipio","summary":"Tuditanus leads six hundred men out of the smaller camp through the enemy to Canusium; the rest of both camps surrender. Maharbal urges an immediate march on Rome and tells Hannibal he knows how to win a victory but not how to use one; Livy says that day's delay saved the City. The battlefield is described in grim detail, and the Apulian woman Busa feeds the fugitives at Canusium. There the young Publius Scipio, learning that Metellus and other nobles plan to abandon Italy, bursts in with drawn sword and makes them swear not to desert the republic; Varro gathers the remnants, while Rome believes everything lost.","ideas":["Tuditanus breaks out with shields shifted to the right","Maharbal's rebuke and the delay that saved Rome","Scipio's oath forced on the would-be deserters","Livy says no other people could have survived such a blow"],"terms":[]},{"i":229,"j":237,"loc":"Book XXII 55–58","title":"Rome steadies itself; Hannibal offers ransom","summary":"The praetors convene the senate, and Fabius proposes sending horsemen for news, keeping women indoors, silencing lamentation and guarding the gates. Varro's dispatch confirms the disaster; mourning is limited to thirty days, and word comes of a Punic fleet threatening Sicily. Two Vestals are convicted of unchastity, Fabius Pictor is sent to Delphi, and a Gallic and a Greek couple are buried alive, a rite Livy calls alien to Rome. Marcus Junius is named dictator, youths and eight thousand slaves are enlisted, and Hannibal, releasing the allies, offers to let the Roman prisoners be ransomed, sending ten envoys and Carthalo, who is ordered out of Roman territory.","ideas":["Fabius restores order in the panicked City","The Vestal scandal treated as a portent; human sacrifice in the Cattle Market","Slaves are armed rather than prisoners redeemed","Hannibal claims to fight for honour and dominion, not extermination"],"terms":[{"t":"Vestals","d":"Priestesses of Vesta bound to chastity, buried alive if convicted of breaking it."}]},{"i":238,"j":244,"loc":"Book XXII 59–61","title":"The prisoners refused; the allies revolt","summary":"The prisoners' spokesman pleads that they fought until nightfall and surrendered only when cut off, compares their cost to that of the armed slaves, and begs not to be left to Hannibal. Titus Manlius Torquatus answers that they refused to follow Tuditanus's sally, even obstructed it, and surrendered the camp without a fight, and so deserve no ransom. The senate, mindful of tradition, the treasury and Hannibal's need of money, refuses; the envoy who evaded his oath is sent back under guard, and Livy records a divergent version. Many allies now revolt, from Campania to the Greek cities and the Cisalpine Gauls, yet no one speaks of peace, and the people thank Varro for not despairing of the state.","ideas":["The plea for ransom versus Manlius's stern rebuttal","Rome's traditional disregard for soldiers who surrender","The list of peoples who go over to Hannibal","Varro thanked rather than punished, unlike a Carthaginian general"],"terms":[]},{"i":245,"j":251,"loc":"Summary of Book XXII","title":"Summary of Book Twenty-Two","summary":"An ancient abstract recapitulates the book: Hannibal's march through the marshes and lost eye, Flaminius's defiance of the omens and his death at Lake Thrasymennus, and the Sacred Spring vowed afterwards. It recounts Fabius's delaying strategy, Minucius's equal command, rescue and submission, and the escape by the burning oxen. It then summarizes Cannae and its toll, Scipio's oath forced on the young nobles, the arming of eight thousand slaves, and the refusal to ransom the prisoners. It closes with the grief at Rome and the thanks given to Varro.","ideas":["A compressed retelling of Trasimene, Fabius and Cannae","The summary names Atherbal as the officer whose pledge Hannibal broke","Scipio is identified as the later Africanus"],"terms":[{"t":"periocha","d":"An ancient short summary of a book of Livy."}]},{"i":252,"j":262,"loc":"Book XXIII summary, 1–6","title":"After Cannae: Capua turns toward Hannibal","summary":"Book XXIII opens with Hannibal taking Compsa and failing before Naples, then turning to luxurious Capua, where Pacuvius Calavius shuts the senators in their house and lets the commons judge them one by one; unable to agree on replacements, the people keep the old senate, which thereafter courts popular favour. Campanian envoys find the consul Varro at Venusia, and he frankly admits that everything was lost at Cannae and asks Capua to carry the war. Vibius Virius argues on the way home that Capua can now win the sovereignty of Italy, and the majority resolves to send the same envoys to Hannibal.","ideas":["Pacuvius saves the senate by a staged trial and makes it his dependant","Varro's candid account of Roman losses breeds contempt rather than loyalty","Capua hopes to inherit the rule of Italy once Hannibal departs","Livy doubts the story of a Campanian demand for a consulship"],"terms":[{"t":"senate of Capua","d":"The Campanian council that Pacuvius preserves but reduces to flattering the commons."}]},{"i":263,"j":266,"loc":"Book XXIII 7–10","title":"Hannibal in Capua; Perolla and Decius Magius","summary":"Capua makes a treaty with Hannibal guaranteeing its own laws and magistrates, and the commons suffocate Roman citizens in the baths. Decius Magius openly opposes admitting the Carthaginians and walks calmly in the forum while the city greets Hannibal. At a banquet, Perolla, son of Pacuvius, reveals to his father a sword with which he means to kill Hannibal, but is talked out of it by his father's pleas about the guest-bond and the danger. Hannibal has the senate surrender Magius, who is shipped in chains toward Carthage, driven by storm to Cyrene, freed by Ptolemy, and chooses to stay in Egypt.","ideas":["Treaty terms leave Capua formally free of Carthaginian command","Perolla's aborted plot to kill Hannibal at the banquet","Magius's arrest shows the hollowness of Capua's new liberty"],"terms":[{"t":"hospitium (guest-friendship)","d":"The sacred bond of host and guest that Pacuvius invokes against his son's plan."}]},{"i":267,"j":270,"loc":"Book XXIII 11–14","title":"The Delphic reply and debate at Carthage","summary":"Fabius Pictor returns from Delphi with the oracle's instructions for supplication and an offering to Apollo after victory. At Carthage Mago reports Hannibal's victories and pours out the gold rings of fallen Roman knights, asking for men, money and corn. Himilco of the Barcine faction taunts Hanno, who replies that a victor should not need supplies, that no Latin city or Roman tribe has defected, and that Rome has not sought peace, so the war is as entire as at its start. Few heed him; reinforcements are voted but sent slowly, while at Rome the dictator Junius Pera arms debtors and criminals, and Marcellus moves to Nola, whose senate stalls the commons' wish to revolt.","ideas":["The heap of gold rings as proof of Cannae","Hanno: a moment of strength is the time to make peace","Carthage executes its decrees sluggishly in prosperity","Rome enlists convicts and debtors armed with Gallic spoils"],"terms":[{"t":"Barcine faction","d":"The party at Carthage attached to Hamilcar's family and Hannibal, opposed by Hanno."}]},{"i":271,"j":276,"loc":"Book XXIII 15–20","title":"Nola, Casilinum and the softening at Capua","summary":"Hannibal starves Nuceria into surrender and burns it, while Marcellus wins over the wavering Nolan horseman Lucius Bantius with praise and gifts. Learning of a plot by the Nolan commons, Marcellus sallies from three gates and checks Hannibal, which Livy calls perhaps the greatest success of the war, then executes over seventy traitors. Hannibal takes Acerrae but is held off at Casilinum by a small garrison of Praenestines and Perusians, and winters at Capua, where luxury unnerves his army. In spring the starving garrison, fed for a time by casks and nuts floated down the Vulturnus, ransoms itself; the loyal Petelini are told Rome can no longer protect them.","ideas":["Marcellus shows Hannibal can be resisted","Capua's pleasures are judged a worse error than not marching on Rome","Casilinum's famine: shield leather, mice and sown turnips","Rome admits it cannot defend distant allies"],"terms":[{"t":"corona muralis (mural crown)","d":"A gold crown for the first man over an enemy wall, promised by Hannibal at Casilinum."}]},{"i":277,"j":282,"loc":"Book XXIII 21–25","title":"Empty treasury, new senators, Postumius destroyed","summary":"Commanders in Sicily and Sardinia report they lack pay and corn and are told to provide for themselves; Hiero supplies Sicily. A financial board is created, and when Spurius Carvilius proposes admitting two senators from each Latin state, Fabius Maximus has the idea buried in silence. Marcus Fabius Buteo, named a second dictator to revise the roll, fills 177 places by rank and service and at once resigns. News then comes that the consul-elect Lucius Postumius and his army were crushed by sawn trees in the Litana forest, his skull made a Boian drinking cup; Gracchus steadies the senate, Gaul is postponed, and the Cannae survivors are sent to Sicily.","ideas":["Rome refuses to admit Latins to its senate","Buteo fills the senate by office held, not personal choice","The ambush in the Litana forest","Cannae troops banished to Sicily for the war's duration"],"terms":[{"t":"lectio senatus","d":"The revision of the senate roll, here carried out by a dictator instead of censors."}]},{"i":283,"j":288,"loc":"Book XXIII 26–30","title":"Spain held, Bruttium lost, year's end","summary":"In Spain Hasdrubal crushes a Tartessian revolt but is ordered to march for Italy, and warns Carthage that Spain will be lost if he leaves; Himilco is sent to replace him. The Scipios unite and defeat him near Ibera, his Spanish centre giving way at once, which ends his hope of reaching Italy. In Italy Petilia falls after a starving siege, Consentia and most of Croton are taken, and Locri revolts, leaving Rhegium loyal; in Sicily Hiero's son Gelo goes over to Carthage but dies suddenly. The year closes with temple dedications, games, the consul Gracchus taking office, and Marcellus continued as proconsul for his successes.","ideas":["Romans fight in Spain as if defending Rome itself","Spanish troops unwilling to be dragged into Italy","Rome rejoices chiefly that Hasdrubal is stopped","Gelo's defection and convenient death"],"terms":[]},{"i":289,"j":292,"loc":"Book XXIII 31–34","title":"Elections, Mago diverted, Philip's treaty","summary":"A double tax is decreed, the armies are exchanged, and Marcellus is elected consul, but thunder at his entry leads to his abdication and Fabius Maximus is chosen in his place. Carthage, hearing of losses in Spain and of Sardinian discontent under Hampsicora, sends Mago to Spain and Hasdrubal the Bald to Sardinia. Philip of Macedon's envoy Xenophanes slips through Roman lines by claiming to seek a Roman alliance and makes a treaty with Hannibal dividing Italy and Greece between them. On the return voyage the envoys are captured, their story collapses, and Hannibal's letter and the treaty are found; Titus Manlius Torquatus is sent to Sardinia.","ideas":["Two plebeian consuls said to displease the gods","Treaty: Italy to Carthage, Greek coasts to Philip","Xenophanes's ruse succeeds once and then fails","Hasdrubal's fleet storm-driven to the Balearic Islands"],"terms":[{"t":"vitio creatus","d":"An election held to be flawed by bad omens, here thunder, forcing the magistrate to resign."}]},{"i":293,"j":297,"loc":"Book XXIII 35–39","title":"Hamae, the siege of Cumae, Macedonian plans","summary":"Gracchus trains his army of volunteer slaves to forget their former status, then, warned by Cumae, surprises the Campanian force at the nocturnal rite at Hamae and kills its magistrate Marius Alfius. Hannibal besieges Cumae, but Gracchus burns his great siege tower from a higher one built on the wall and drives him back to Tifata, while other Roman commanders win in Lucania and among the Hirpini. The captured Macedonian envoys reach Rome, and the senate prepares a fleet to keep Philip occupied at home. One captive ship escapes to Philip, who sends a second embassy, but the summer passes before he acts; Fabius retakes several towns and guards Nola.","ideas":["Concord between freeborn and slave soldiers","Surprise attack during a festival","Rome answers a new threat by taking the offensive","The loss of one ship delays a Macedonian war"],"terms":[{"t":"volones (volunteer slaves)","d":"Slaves bought by the state and armed after Cannae, serving under Gracchus."},{"t":"medix tuticus","d":"The title of Capua's chief magistrate."}]},{"i":298,"j":302,"loc":"Book XXIII 40–44","title":"Sardinia subdued; Hannibal returns to Nola","summary":"Manlius defeats Hampsicora's son, then the joint Sardinian and Carthaginian army, capturing Hasdrubal, Mago and Hanno; Hampsicora kills himself and the island submits. Otacilius takes seven Carthaginian ships, and Marcellus raids Samnium from Nola so harshly that the Hirpini and Samnites complain to Hannibal that Marcellus seems the victor of Cannae. Hannibal answers sharply and marches on Nola, where Hanno offers the Nolan leaders terms if they surrender Marcellus. Herennius Bassus refuses, citing long friendship with Rome, and Marcellus sallies against the besiegers until a storm parts the armies.","ideas":["The capture of three Carthaginian commanders in Sardinia","Allies complain they are left undefended","Hanno contrasts Roman harshness with Hannibal's clemency","Nola's nobles keep faith with Rome"],"terms":[]},{"i":303,"j":308,"loc":"Book XXIII 45–49","title":"Second victory at Nola; Spain financed","summary":"Marcellus tells his men the enemy has been ruined by Capuan luxury, while Hannibal reproaches his troops for faltering; the Carthaginians are routed with over five thousand dead, and 1,272 Numidian and Spanish horsemen desert to Rome. Hannibal winters near Arpi, and in a challenge before Capua the Campanian Taurea backs away from Claudius Asellus in a hollow lane. The Scipios write that their army lacks clothing and corn, and since the treasury is empty, companies of contractors supply Spain on credit. The Scipios then relieve Illiturgi and win again at Intibili, bringing most of Spain over to Rome.","ideas":["Capua as Hannibal's Cannae","The single combat that never quite happens","Private credit sustains the state","Spanish successes outweigh Italian ones this year"],"terms":[{"t":"spolia opima","d":"Spoils taken from an enemy leader in single combat, which Taurea offers to stake."},{"t":"publicani","d":"Contractors who supply the Spanish army on condition the state bears losses at sea."}]},{"i":309,"j":316,"loc":"Book XXIV summary, 1–3","title":"Locri surrenders, Croton abandoned","summary":"Book XXIV opens with Hanno and the Bruttians attacking the Greek cities: Locri, its people cut off in the fields, surrenders and gains a treaty of equal alliance, and the Roman garrison is shipped to Rhegium. The Bruttians, resenting the loss of plunder, take Croton with the help of its commons, but its nobles hold the citadel. Livy describes the ruined city and the rich, sacred temple of Juno Lacinia nearby. The Crotonians refuse to accept a Bruttian colony and are instead removed to Locri; in Apulia Gracchus and Hannibal skirmish through the winter.","ideas":["Throughout Italy the commons favour Carthage, the nobles Rome","Carthage acts as umpire rather than plunderer of the Greek cities","The temple and sacred flocks of Juno Lacinia","Croton prefers exile to Bruttian customs and language"],"terms":[]},{"i":317,"j":320,"loc":"Book XXIV 4–7","title":"Hieronymus of Syracuse: tyranny and murder","summary":"Hiero dies, leaving his fifteen-year-old grandson Hieronymus under guardians, and Andranodorus soon gathers power to himself. Hieronymus adopts the purple, diadem and guards of a tyrant, and when a plot is revealed the tortured Theodotus shields his accomplices by naming the pro-Roman Thraso, who is executed. The king mocks Roman envoys, allies with Hannibal through Hippocrates and Epicydes, and demands all Sicily for himself. At Leontini the conspirators kill him in a narrow street while Dinomenes holds back the crowd; at Rome Fabius begins the consular elections.","ideas":["Hiero had thought of leaving Syracuse free","Theodotus's false confession removes Rome's friend","Hieronymus's shifting demands of Carthage","Assassination at Leontini"],"terms":[]},{"i":321,"j":325,"loc":"Book XXIV 8–12","title":"Fabius's election speech and Rome's preparations","summary":"Fabius halts the vote and argues that consuls must be chosen as generals fit to face Hannibal, faulting the elected Otacilius for failing to stop supplies to Carthage's army. The century votes again, electing Fabius for a fourth time and Marcellus for a third, and Livy notes the people's approval of Fabius's motives. Prodigies are expiated, eighteen legions are fixed, and for the first time a Roman fleet is manned with sailors paid for by private citizens according to wealth. Alarmed, Capua calls in Hannibal, who returns to Tifata and moves toward Puteoli.","ideas":["Choose consuls as you would choose generals before battle","The image of the pilot needed in a storm","Citizens fund sailors in proportion to property","A long list of reported prodigies"],"terms":[{"t":"centuria praerogativa","d":"The century chosen by lot to vote first, whose choice usually swayed the rest."}]},{"i":326,"j":333,"loc":"Book XXIV 13–20","title":"Beneventum: freedom for the slave-soldiers","summary":"Young Tarentines promise Hannibal their city, and he fails at Puteoli and again at Nola. At Beneventum Gracchus promises freedom to slaves who bring back an enemy head, then, seeing them slowed by the heads, orders them dropped; they rout Hanno, Gracchus frees them all, and shames the laggards by making them eat standing. At Rome the censors degrade those who planned to abandon Italy after Cannae, and citizens, widows and soldiers lend money to the state. Fabius and Marcellus take Casilinum, Fabius ravages Samnium, and Hannibal's mild march to Tarentum fails because Marcus Livius has secured the city.","ideas":["Heads as the price of liberty hamper the fight","The banquet of the freed slaves in Beneventum's streets","Censors punish disloyalty and evasion of oaths","Public credit supported by private generosity"],"terms":[{"t":"censors","d":"Magistrates who here review citizens' conduct and strike offenders from tribe and equestrian rank."}]},{"i":334,"j":339,"loc":"Book XXIV 21–26","title":"Syracuse after the tyrant: liberty and bloodshed","summary":"Theodotus and Sosis ride into Syracuse with the king's bloodied robe, and the people arm themselves for liberty. Polyaenus urges restraint, and Andranodorus, despite his wife Demarata's urging to hold on, yields the island and treasury and is elected a magistrate. Hippocrates and Epicydes stir soldiers and deserters against the nobles, and Andranodorus plots again until the actor Ariston betrays him and he and Themistus are killed in the senate house. The people then decree death for the whole royal house, and Heraclea and her daughters are murdered just before a message of mercy arrives.","ideas":["The crowd is either servile or tyrannical, Livy says","Royal wives blamed for the hunger for kingship","Heraclea's plea for her innocent daughters","Pity arriving too late turns to anger"],"terms":[{"t":"Achradina, Insula, Tycha, Hexapylum","d":"Districts and gate of Syracuse in which the struggle unfolds."}]},{"i":340,"j":345,"loc":"Book XXIV 27–32","title":"Hippocrates and Epicydes seize Syracuse","summary":"The crowd elects Hippocrates and Epicydes as magistrates, while the council, persuaded by Apollonides that unity matters most, makes peace with Rome. Hippocrates, sent to Leontini, attacks Roman posts, and when Leontini defies Syracuse, Marcellus storms it and scourges and beheads two thousand deserters. Rumours of a massacre and a forged letter turn the Syracusan army against its commanders, and Cretan troops shelter the two brothers. They enter Syracuse through the Hexapylum, kill the magistrates, free the slaves, and the city falls back into servitude.","ideas":["Apollonides: civil division is worse than either alliance","The false report of a massacre at Leontini","A forged letter to Marcellus inflames the troops","Brief liberty ends in renewed servitude"],"terms":[]},{"i":346,"j":352,"loc":"Book XXIV 33–39","title":"Archimedes, Himilco, and the massacre at Enna","summary":"Epicydes rebuffs Roman envoys, and Marcellus attacks Syracuse by sea and land, but Archimedes' engines hurl stones, shoot through loopholes and lift ships by grapple and drop them, so the Romans turn to blockade. Marcellus takes Megara, and Himilco lands a large army, retakes Agrigentum, and with Hippocrates rouses Sicily, though Marcellus cuts off Hippocrates' infantry at Acrillae. Bomilcar's fleet withdraws to Africa and Murgantia betrays its garrison. At Enna the commander Pinarius, pressed to hand over the keys, has his soldiers slaughter the assembled townspeople in the theatre, and the outrage drives more of Sicily to Carthage.","ideas":["One man's engineering defeats a Roman assault","Siege turned into blockade","Pinarius's pre-emptive massacre, 'atrocious or unavoidable'","Enna's sacredness to Ceres and Proserpine deepens the shock"],"terms":[{"t":"tolleno","d":"A counterweighted beam used by Archimedes to hoist ships by an iron grapple."}]},{"i":353,"j":356,"loc":"Book XXIV 40–43","title":"Philip routed at Apollonia; Spanish victories","summary":"Philip takes Oricum, but Marcus Valerius retakes it, and Naevius Crista slips into Apollonia and surprises the Macedonian camp at night, sending Philip fleeing half-dressed; he burns his ships and retreats overland. In Spain the Scipios recover from early setbacks, relieve Illiturgi and Bigerra, and win at Munda and Auringis, Gnaeus fighting from a litter after a wound. Ashamed that Saguntum had been held by the enemy for eight years, they retake it and enslave the Turdetani. At Rome the tribune Metellus prosecutes the censors, and Fabius holds elections that make his son consul with Gracchus.","ideas":["A night attack wrecks Philip's first campaign","Carthage keeps raising new armies in Spain","Saguntum restored to its survivors","Censors shielded from prosecution while in office"],"terms":[]},{"i":357,"j":363,"loc":"Book XXIV 44–49","title":"Arpi recovered; Syphax and Masinissa","summary":"Provinces are assigned, and the elder Fabius, riding past his son's lictors, is told to dismount and praises him for asserting his office. The elder Fabius urges that Dasius Altinius, who offers to betray Arpi, be held in custody rather than punished, so as not to deter allies from returning, while Hannibal burns Altinius's family alive. The consul takes Arpi on a rainy night, and the townsmen and Spanish soldiers come over. The Scipios ally with the Numidian Syphax, whose infantry the centurion Statorius trains, but Carthage enlists Gala, whose young son Masinissa defeats Syphax; the Celtiberians become the first mercenaries in a Roman camp.","ideas":["A father honours his son's consular authority","Leniency to returning defectors as policy","Rain covers the capture of Arpi","Rome reaches into Africa through Syphax"],"terms":[{"t":"lictors and fasces","d":"Attendants bearing rods that mark a magistrate's power, to which even a father must defer."}]},{"i":364,"j":372,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 1–4","title":"Foreign rites, elections, and the Posthumius fraud","summary":"Book XXV opens with its summary of the years 213–212 B.C. Hannibal waits near Tarentum hoping for its betrayal, while at Rome a flood of foreign rites and soothsayers prompts the senate to have the praetor Marcus Atilius call in books of divination and forbid foreign sacrifice in public places. A dictator holds elections; Fulvius Flaccus and Appius Claudius become consuls, and the young Publius Scipio wins the curule aedileship despite being under the legal age. The revenue farmer Marcus Posthumius Pyrgensis, who sank old ships to claim false losses from the state, is prosecuted by the tribunes; when his fellow contractors break up the assembly by force, the senate condemns the violence and Posthumius, indicted capitally, flees into exile.","ideas":["War-weary Rome turns to foreign superstitions, which the senate suppresses","Scipio's retort that the people's wish makes him old enough","Twenty-three legions are raised for the year","Contractors' shipwreck fraud and their riot end in exile"],"terms":[{"t":"publicani (revenue farmers)","d":"Private contractors who supplied the armies and collected public revenues; here the state bore the risk of storm losses, which they exploited."},{"t":"aqua et igni interdictio","d":"Interdiction from water and fire, the formula of exile pronounced against Posthumius if he failed to appear."}]},{"i":373,"j":375,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 5–7","title":"The Cannae survivors plead with Marcellus","summary":"The young Publius Licinius Crassus is elected chief pontiff over two senior consulars, and the senate scours the countryside for recruits, even under seventeen. The disgraced survivors of Cannae, kept in Sicily, send envoys to Marcellus; their spokesman argues that the fault at Cannae was the generals', that past armies who fled were given the chance to redeem themselves, and asks only to be put to the hardest service. Marcellus refers the matter to the senate, which lets him use them but forbids rewards, discharge, or return to Italy while the enemy remains. After prodigies and expiations, the Tarentine hostages at Rome escape with Phileas, are recaptured at Tarracina, scourged, and thrown from the rock.","ideas":["Plea that soldiers should not bear the shame their commanders escaped","Precedents of the Allia and the Caudine Forks","Senate grants service but no honour to the Cannae legions","Execution of the Tarentine hostages sets up Tarentum's revolt"],"terms":[{"t":"pontifex maximus (chief pontiff)","d":"Head of the college of pontiffs; Crassus is notable for winning it without having held a curule office."}]},{"i":376,"j":380,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 8–12","title":"Hannibal takes Tarentum; the Apollinarian games","summary":"Angered by the hostages' execution, Tarentine youths led by Nico and Philemenus agree terms with Hannibal: freedom and their own laws in return for the Roman garrison. Philemenus, using his habit of night hunting, gets a gate opened for a huge boar and kills the guard, while Nico admits Hannibal at the Temenid gate; the Romans are cut down and their houses, unmarked by Tarentine names, plundered. The Romans hold the citadel on its peninsula, so Hannibal walls it off and has the Tarentine fleet hauled on wagons through the city to the open sea. At Rome the prophecies of the seer Marcius, one having foretold Cannae, lead to the founding of the Apollinarian games for victory.","ideas":["Betrayal of Tarentum by the hunting ruse","Names written on doors to spare Tarentine houses","Ships carried overland across the city to blockade the citadel","Marcius's verses establish games to Apollo"],"terms":[{"t":"ludi Apollinares (Apollinarian games)","d":"Games vowed to Apollo on Marcius's prophecy, Livy says for victory and not, as commonly thought, for health."},{"t":"decemviri sacris faciundis","d":"The board of ten in charge of the sacred books, who sacrificed to Apollo and Latona in the Greek manner."}]},{"i":381,"j":385,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 13–17","title":"Hanno's camp stormed; death of Gracchus","summary":"Starving Capua asks Hannibal for grain, and Hanno gathers supplies in a hill camp near Beneventum, but the Campanians are slow to collect it. The consul Fulvius attacks the camp; when he orders a retreat, the Pelignian Vibius Accuaeus and the centurion Titus Pedanius hurl standards over the rampart and the camp is taken with its grain and wagons. Meanwhile Metapontum and Thurii go over to Carthage, Thurii shutting its gates on the Roman garrison after an ambush. Tiberius Gracchus, warned by an omen of snakes eating the sacrificial liver, is lured by the Lucanian Flavius to a parley and killed fighting in an ambush; Livy records divergent accounts of his death and of Hannibal's honourable funeral for him.","ideas":["Standards thrown into the enemy camp force the assault","Thurii and Metapontum defect","Gracchus betrayed by his guest-friend Flavius","Livy weighs rival versions of Gracchus's death"],"terms":[{"t":"haruspices (aruspices)","d":"Diviners who read entrails; they warned Gracchus the snake prodigy concerned the general and hidden plots."}]},{"i":386,"j":390,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 18–22","title":"Crispinus and Badius; Roman disasters; Capua encircled","summary":"The Campanians win a skirmish, but the Roman Crispinus, challenged by his former guest Badius, wounds him in single combat. Hannibal comes to Capua for an indecisive cavalry battle; the consuls slip away in two directions, and Hannibal destroys the army of the ex-centurion Centenius Penula in Lucania, then routs the undisciplined praetor Cneius Fulvius at Herdonia, killing all but two thousand of eighteen thousand. Rome collects the remnants and recovers the deserting volunteer slaves. Three Roman armies ring Capua with a double ditch and rampart, offer terms to Campanians who leave by the Ides of March, and are scorned; Hannibal, busy at Tarentum and Brundusium, sends Capua only proud assurances.","ideas":["Private hospitality breaks under public war in the duel","A centurion's boast that he can beat Hannibal ends in slaughter","Fulvius flees Herdonia with two hundred horse","Capua is fully invested"],"terms":[{"t":"volones (volunteer slaves)","d":"Slaves who enlisted under Gracchus for freedom; they left their standards after his death and were rounded up."},{"t":"circumvallation","d":"The ring of ditch, rampart and forts by which the consuls sealed Capua."}]},{"i":391,"j":394,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 23–26","title":"Marcellus enters Syracuse during Diana's festival","summary":"After a betrayal plot is exposed and its eighty conspirators killed, a Roman negotiating near the tower Galeagra counts the stones and judges the wall low enough for ladders. During a three-day festival of Diana, when the city is drunk, Marcellus sends a thousand men over the wall, forces the Hexapylos, and seizes Epipolae; looking down on the city, he weeps at its fame and its imminent ruin. Neapolis and Tycha are plundered without bloodshed, and the Euryalus fort is surrendered, while Achradina and the Island hold out. A relieving attack by Hippocrates and Himilco fails, and a plague destroys the Carthaginian army and its generals.","ideas":["Wall height estimated by counting courses of stone","Night escalade while the Syracusans feast","Marcellus's tears over Syracuse","Plague wipes out the Carthaginian land army"],"terms":[{"t":"Epipolae","d":"The high ground of Syracuse, taken once the trumpets sounded from the walls."},{"t":"Achradina and Nasos","d":"The inner city and the Island, the last districts held by Epicydes' men and the deserters."}]},{"i":395,"j":399,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 27–31","title":"Surrender of Syracuse and death of Archimedes","summary":"Bomilcar's great relief fleet turns away at Pachynum without fighting. The Sicilian army negotiates terms with Marcellus, and within the city Epicydes' prefects are killed and envoys sent pleading that the revolt was the tyrants', not Syracuse's, and asking that the city be spared as a monument. Fearing betrayal, the deserters and mercenaries massacre the new praetors, but the Spanish prefect Mericus is won over and admits Romans near the fountain of Arethusa while a landing takes the Island. Marcellus replies that he besieged the city to free it from the deserters, secures the royal treasure, and gives the city up to plunder. Archimedes, intent on figures drawn in the dust, is killed by a soldier who does not know him, to Marcellus's grief; Otacilius's raid on Utica brings grain that averts famine.","ideas":["Syracusans blame Hieronymus and his creatures for the revolt","Mercenaries and deserters turn on the city","Mericus betrays the Achradina quarter","Archimedes killed amid the sack"],"terms":[{"t":"transfugae (deserters)","d":"Roman deserters in Syracuse who, expecting no pardon, resisted surrender to the end."}]},{"i":400,"j":404,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 32–36","title":"The two Scipios destroyed in Spain","summary":"The Scipios decide to end the Spanish war, split their forces, and rely on twenty thousand hired Celtiberians. Hasdrubal son of Hamilcar bribes the Celtiberians to go home, leaving Cneius too weak, and Livy warns generals never to depend on foreign auxiliaries. Publius, harried by Masinissa's Numidians, marches out at night to stop Indibilis, is surrounded by three enemies, and is killed by a lance. The victorious Carthaginian armies join against Cneius, who retreats to a bare hill, builds a rampart of packsaddles and baggage, and is overrun; he dies twenty-nine days after his brother, mourned in Spain for his justice.","ideas":["Bribed Celtiberians desert Cneius Scipio","Livy's lesson against trusting foreign auxiliaries","Masinissa's cavalry wears down Publius Scipio","The baggage rampart on the hill falls"],"terms":[{"t":"auxilia (auxiliaries)","d":"Foreign allied troops; their desertion here prompts Livy to urge keeping one's own forces the stronger part."}]},{"i":405,"j":407,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 37–39","title":"Lucius Marcius saves Roman Spain","summary":"Lucius Marcius, a Roman knight trained by Cneius Scipio, gathers the survivors and is elected commander by the soldiers. When Hasdrubal son of Gisgo approaches, the men's weeping for the Scipios turns to fury and they rout the careless enemy, though Marcius holds them back from pursuit. In a speech he explains that men are least on guard against what they do not fear, and proposes a night attack on the overconfident Carthaginians. The Romans burn the nearer camp, cut down fugitives with a hidden cohort, and take the second camp by day; Livy gives widely differing casualty figures from Claudius, Valerius Antias and Piso, and reports the tale of flame from Marcius's head and the Marcian shield.","ideas":["Soldiers elect a knight as general","Grief converted into a sudden sally","Boldest counsels safest in desperate straits","Two enemy camps taken in a night and a day"],"terms":[{"t":"eques Romanus (Roman knight)","d":"Marcius's rank, below the senatorial class, stressing that the command fell to him by merit and the army's vote."}]},{"i":408,"j":410,"loc":"Book XXV, chs. 40–41","title":"Syracusan spoils, Mutines, and the battle at Himera","summary":"Marcellus settles Sicily and sends Syracuse's statues and paintings to Rome, which Livy names as the origin of Roman admiration for Greek art and of the later plundering of temples. At Agrigentum the Libyphoenician Mutines, trained by Hannibal, leads Numidian cavalry with great success, but while he is away calming a mutiny, the jealous Hanno insists on battle. The Numidians, resenting their commander's removal, tell Marcellus they will stand aside; the Carthaginians are routed at the river Himera in Marcellus's last Sicilian battle. Claudius returns to Rome to elect Cneius Fulvius Centumalus and Publius Sulpicius Galba consuls, and the year ends.","ideas":["Syracusan art in Rome begins a taste for Greek works and for plunder","Hanno's jealousy of the half-African Mutines","Numidian defection decides the battle","Elections for the following year"],"terms":[{"t":"Libyphoenician","d":"A person of mixed Libyan and Phoenician descent, the origin Hanno sneers at in Mutines."}]},{"i":411,"j":418,"loc":"Book XXVI, summary and chapters 1–3","title":"Commands assigned; Cneius Fulvius tried and exiled","summary":"Book XXVI opens with its summary and the arrangements of the new consuls, Cneius Fulvius Centumalus and Publius Sulpicius Galba: the proconsuls Quintus Fulvius and Appius Claudius are kept before Capua until it falls, and twenty-three legions are deployed by land and sea. A letter from Lucius Marcius in Spain, styling himself propraetor, offends the senate as a precedent of armies choosing their own generals, and the people are asked to choose a commander for Spain. The tribune Caius Sempronius Blaesus prosecutes Cneius Fulvius for losing his army in Apulia, charging that he corrupted his legions and was first to flee. Fulvius blames his soldiers, but when witnesses swear the panic began with him the charge becomes capital, and he goes into exile at Tarquinii.","ideas":["Capua's recapture is pursued as an example to other defecting states","Marcius's self-assumed title raises the issue of generals chosen by armies","Disgraced legions are punished while their general escapes, Blaesus complains","Cneius Fulvius's trial ends in voluntary exile declared legal"],"terms":[{"t":"propraetor","d":"A commander holding a praetor's authority outside his year of office, a title Marcius took without a grant from people or senate."},{"t":"capital trial","d":"A prosecution putting the accused's life or citizenship at stake, as opposed to one for a fine."}]},{"i":419,"j":424,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 4–9","title":"Hannibal fails at Capua and marches on Rome","summary":"Capua is starved under close blockade; the Romans counter Campanian cavalry by training light infantry to ride behind horsemen and leap down, the origin Livy gives for the velites. Hannibal comes from Bruttium and attacks the Roman lines while the Campanians sally, but Navius's charge with a standard and the defence of the rampart repulse him, though Appius Claudius is wounded. Unable to break through, Hannibal decides to strike at Rome itself, sending word to Capua to hold out. In the senate Fabius Maximus opposes abandoning Capua, and Valerius Flaccus's middle course sends Quintus Fulvius with 15,000 foot to Rome while Hannibal ravages the Latin way and camps eight miles from the city amid panic.","ideas":["Mounted light infantry give the Romans cavalry superiority before Capua","Hannibal's combined assault with the Campanians on the siege lines is beaten back","Hannibal marches on Rome hoping to draw the generals off Capua","Rome divides its forces: Fulvius comes home, Claudius keeps the siege"],"terms":[{"t":"velites","d":"Light-armed skirmishers of the legion, whose institution Livy links to this expedient at Capua."},{"t":"Medixtuticus","d":"The chief magistrate of Capua, held in its last year by the obscure Seppius Lesius."}]},{"i":425,"j":427,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 10–12","title":"Hannibal at the gates; Capua abandoned","summary":"Fulvius Flaccus enters Rome and camps between the Esquiline and Colline gates, while Hannibal camps on the Anio and rides up to view the walls; a false cry that the Aventine is taken causes chaos in the streets. Two days running, hail and rain stop the armies from fighting, and Hannibal learns that troops have marched out for Spain and that the land under his camp has sold at full price. He withdraws, plunders the temple of Feronia, and heads south to Rhegium; Livy notes the dispute over his route. At Capua the abandoned Campanians lose heart, the Punic commanders write reproaching Hannibal, and Numidian letter-bearers betrayed by a woman are scourged, mutilated and sent back into the city.","ideas":["Storms twice prevent a battle with Rome as the prize","Rome's confidence: reinforcements leave for Spain, land under Hannibal's camp sells","Hannibal retreats, plundering Feronia's temple","Capua, deserted, is crushed in spirit by the punishment of the Numidians"],"terms":[{"t":"Anio","d":"River three miles from Rome where Hannibal pitched his camp."},{"t":"Feronia","d":"Italian goddess whose rich grove and temple Hannibal stripped on his withdrawal."}]},{"i":428,"j":431,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 13–16","title":"Fall of Capua and Fulvius's punishments","summary":"Forced to meet, the Campanian senate hears Vibius Virrius, instigator of the revolt, argue that Rome's relentless siege proves no mercy can be expected and invite the senators to a last banquet and poison. About twenty-seven join him and die before the gates open; the rest surrender, and the senators are put in chains at Cales and Teanum. Against Appius's wish to refer the matter to Rome, Fulvius rides out and has them scourged and beheaded, pocketing the senate's letter unread at Cales; Jubellius Taurea kills himself at his feet, though Livy reports other versions. Eighty senators die, the people are sold, and Capua survives only as a settlement without magistrates, senate or assembly, governed by an annual prefect.","ideas":["Virrius's speech and the mass suicide of the Campanian senators","Fulvius executes the senators before reading the senate's despatch","Taurea's defiant death, told in variant versions","Capua kept for its land but stripped of political life"],"terms":[{"t":"praefect","d":"An official sent yearly from Rome to administer justice in Capua in place of its own magistrates."},{"t":"lictor","d":"Attendant who carried the rods and axe and carried out a magistrate's sentences."}]},{"i":432,"j":435,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 17–20","title":"Scipio elected to command in Spain","summary":"Caius Nero takes an army to Spain and traps Hasdrubal in a defile, but Hasdrubal spins out surrender talks for days while his troops slip away by night and finally escapes in a mist. At Rome no one offers himself for the Spanish command until Publius Cornelius Scipio, about twenty-four, son of the fallen Publius, stands forward and is chosen unanimously, though misgivings about his youth follow. Livy describes Scipio's cultivated air of divine guidance, his daily solitary visits to the Capitol, and the serpent tale like Alexander's, which he neither affirmed nor denied. Scipio lands at Emporiae, receives embassies at Tarraco with dignity, praises the veterans and Marcius, while the Carthaginian generals winter far apart and a Punic fleet at Tarentum starves its own side.","ideas":["Hasdrubal escapes Nero by prolonging negotiations","The people elect the young Scipio when no other candidate comes forward","Scipio fosters belief in his divine inspiration","The Carthaginian fleet at Tarentum consumes more than it secures"],"terms":[{"t":"proconsul","d":"Commander with a consul's power outside the consulship, the office the people conferred on Scipio for Spain."}]},{"i":436,"j":438,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 21–23","title":"Marcellus's ovation; Torquatus declines the consulship","summary":"Marcellus returns from Sicily and asks for a triumph; since his army was left behind, the senate grants him an ovation, in which the spoils and statues of Syracuse are displayed and the traitors Sosis and Mericus are rewarded. In Sicily a new Carthaginian landing and Mutines's Numidians stir revolts and the discontented soldiers near mutiny, until the praetor Marcus Cornelius restores order. At the consular elections Titus Manlius Torquatus, named by the first century, refuses on account of his weak eyes; the juniors consult their elders and elect Marcellus and Valerius Laevinus. Livy praises this deference against the manners of his own day; praetors are chosen, the Apollinarian games made annual, and prodigies expiated.","ideas":["Marcellus's ovation displays the treasures of Syracuse in Rome","Sicily is disturbed again and pacified by Cornelius","Young voters defer to their elders in choosing consuls","Livy holds this as proof of old Rome's sound judgment"],"terms":[{"t":"ovation","d":"A lesser triumph, granted to Marcellus because his war was not wholly ended and his army absent."},{"t":"centuria praerogativa","d":"The century voting first, here the junior Veturian, whose choice the others usually followed."}]},{"i":439,"j":441,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 24–26","title":"The Aetolian alliance and war with Philip","summary":"Laevinus persuades the Aetolian council into alliance against Philip of Macedon: the Aetolians keep the cities and lands taken as far as Corcyra, the Romans the other booty, and Acarnania is promised to the Aetolians. Philip campaigns against Illyrians, Dardanians and the Maedi in Thrace; the outnumbered Acarnanians, vowing not to return unless victorious, prepare a common tomb, and Philip's approach makes the Aetolians withdraw. Laevinus and Scopas take Anticyra, and Laevinus learns he is consul. At Rome Marcellus refuses business until his colleague arrives and invites the Sicilians to accuse him, while the people grumble at the long war and the warlike consuls.","ideas":["Treaty terms divide land to the Aetolians and booty to Rome","The Acarnanians' oath and epitaph of desperate resistance","Philip shields his kingdom on several fronts","Marcellus faces Sicilian accusers in Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"Aetolian League","d":"Federation of western Greek states, Rome's first ally across the sea in Laevinus's words."}]},{"i":442,"j":447,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 27–32","title":"Arson at Rome and the Sicilians accuse Marcellus","summary":"A fire around the forum, nearly reaching the temple of Vesta, is traced through a slave's evidence to Campanian youths whose parents Fulvius executed, and they are punished. Laevinus brings Campanian petitioners and Sicilians to Rome; the provinces are assigned, and when Marcellus draws Sicily the Sicilians' laments lead the consuls to exchange provinces, Livy noting this sent Marcellus to his fate against Hannibal. In the senate the Sicilians accuse Marcellus of preferring betrayal by low men to surrender by the nobles and of stripping Syracuse; Marcellus replies that Syracuse was an enemy taken by force under the laws of war. Despite Torquatus's sympathy for the city, the senate confirms Marcellus's acts, and the Sicilians beg his pardon and patronage.","ideas":["Campanian arsonists confess and are punished","Marcellus and Laevinus exchange provinces out of regard for the Sicilians","The Sicilians' case against Marcellus and his defence","The senate ratifies his acts and Syracuse seeks his patronage"],"terms":[{"t":"temple of Vesta","d":"Shrine of the eternal fire and pledges of empire, saved from the fire by slaves later freed."},{"t":"patronage (patrocinium)","d":"Protective relationship in which the Syracusans finally placed themselves under Marcellus."}]},{"i":448,"j":451,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 33–36","title":"Campanians judged; senators fund the fleet","summary":"The Campanians plead in the senate but cannot deny their guilt; Marcus Atilius Regulus reports that only two women at Capua served Rome and argues the people must first authorize the senate to judge Roman citizens, as with the Satricani. A bill empowers the senate, which frees the two women and decrees confiscations, sales and removal of the Campanians to set districts, leaving them blaming fate rather than Fulvius. An edict requiring private citizens to supply rowers provokes near-insurrection from an exhausted people. Laevinus proposes that the senators first give nearly all their gold, silver and bronze; their eager example is followed by the knights and commons, and the fleet is manned without compulsion.","ideas":["The people authorize the senate to decide the Campanians' fate","Detailed penalties and resettlement of the Campanians","Popular outcry against the levy of rowers","Laevinus's senatorial contribution sets a voluntary example"],"terms":[{"t":"plebiscite","d":"A measure passed by the commons on a tribune's motion, here granting the senate judgment over the Campanians."},{"t":"bulla","d":"Amulet worn by freeborn boys, which senators were allowed to keep for their sons."}]},{"i":452,"j":455,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 37–40","title":"Balanced fortunes; Salapia, Tarentum, Agrigentum","summary":"Livy weighs the year's gains and losses on both sides, finding hope and fear as finely balanced as at the war's start. Hannibal's harshness to towns he cannot hold alienates others; at Salapia Blasius, after his rival Dasius exposes him before Hannibal, still betrays the town to Marcellus, and 500 Numidian cavalry die fighting, a loss Hannibal never made good. Off Sacriportus the Tarentine fleet defeats Decius Quinctius, slain by Nico, while the citadel garrison routs Tarentine foragers on land. In Sicily Laevinus takes Agrigentum when the slighted Mutines betrays it, Hanno and Epicydes flee to Africa, the island submits, and Laevinus removes the outlaws of Agathyrna to Rhegium.","ideas":["Fortune balanced for Rome and Carthage at this point","Salapia's betrayal costs Hannibal his best cavalry","Tarentines win at sea, the Romans on land","Mutines's betrayal of Agrigentum ends the war in Sicily"],"terms":[{"t":"Numidians","d":"North African horsemen, the core of Hannibal's cavalry and of Mutines's raiding force."}]},{"i":456,"j":461,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 41–46","title":"Scipio's speech and the storming of New Carthage","summary":"At the Iberus Scipio addresses the veterans: Rome has always won after defeats, Italy and Sicily now prosper, the gods and his own mind foretell victory, and the divided Carthaginian generals face his father's fate. He crosses with 25,000 foot and, telling only Laelius, makes for New Carthage, the enemy's store of money, arms and hostages, and Livy describes its site on a peninsula with a lagoon. After urging that one city will deliver all Spain, he beats back Mago's sally, but the scaling ladders prove too short. Learning from fishermen that the lagoon is fordable at ebb tide, aided by a north wind, he sends 500 men through it under Neptune's guidance; they take the walls from behind, the city falls, and Mago surrenders the citadel.","ideas":["Scipio's harangue joins Roman resilience and divine signs","New Carthage chosen as the key to all Spain","Ladders fail against the high walls","The ebb-tide crossing of the lagoon wins the city in a day"],"terms":[{"t":"New Carthage","d":"Carthage's chief base in Spain, holding its treasury, arsenal and Spanish hostages."},{"t":"Mercury's hill","d":"Eminence from which Scipio watched the walls emptying of defenders."}]},{"i":462,"j":466,"loc":"Book XXVI, chapters 47–51","title":"Spoils, hostages, and Scipio's restraint","summary":"Scipio frees the citizens of New Carthage, makes the 2,000 artisans public slaves with hope of freedom, and catalogues vast spoils of engines, gold, silver, grain and ships. A dispute between a legionary and a marine over the mural crown nearly becomes a mutiny, and Scipio awards crowns to both and honours Laelius. Livy notes how sources disagree on the numbers; Scipio treats the hostages kindly and, at the plea of Mandonius's wife, protects the young noblewomen. He returns a beautiful captive untouched to her betrothed, Allucius, giving her ransom as a wedding gift, and Allucius brings 1,400 horsemen; after drilling his forces, Scipio goes to Tarraco while the Carthaginians belittle their loss.","ideas":["Clemency to townsmen and lavish booty at New Carthage","Scipio defuses the quarrel over the mural crown","Livy's distrust of inflated figures in his sources","The continence of Scipio wins Allucius and Spanish goodwill"],"terms":[{"t":"corona muralis (mural crown)","d":"Award for the first soldier over an enemy wall, given here to Trebellius and Digitius jointly."},{"t":"Silenus","d":"Greek historian whom Livy cites among sources disagreeing on the spoils."}]},{"i":467,"j":473,"loc":"Book XXVII, epitome and chapters 1–3","title":"Disaster at Herdonea and the fight at Numistro","summary":"After an epitome of the book, Marcellus takes Salapia, Maronea and Meles, but the proconsul Cneius Fulvius, careless in his camp near Herdonea, is surprised by Hannibal; his cavalry strike the Roman rear and camp, and Fulvius falls with eleven tribunes. Hannibal burns Herdonea and moves its people. Marcellus writes defiantly to the senate, fights Hannibal to a draw at Numistro, and shadows him through Apulia by daylight only. At Capua Flaccus uncovers a Campanian plot, led by the Blosii, to burn the soldiers' straw huts, and executes the conspirators; corn and troops are sent to the Roman citadel at Tarentum.","ideas":["Hannibal's cavalry encirclement destroys a second Fulvius on the same ground","Livy notes that the reported Roman dead range from 7,000 to 13,000","Marcellus answers defeat with immediate pursuit, refusing night marches that invite ambush","Severity at Capua: huts outside the city, a betrayed arson plot, informers freed"],"terms":[{"t":"proconsul","d":"A magistrate whose command is prolonged beyond his year of office, as with Fulvius at Herdonea."}]},{"i":474,"j":477,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 4–7","title":"Election crisis, embassies, and the year's commands","summary":"Marcellus will not leave Hannibal, so the consul Valerius is recalled from Sicily; he reports the island pacified, and Messala's raid on Africa reveals that Masinissa's Numidians and a new fleet are being readied so that Hasdrubal can join Hannibal in Italy. Valerius slips back to Sicily rather than let the people name the dictator, so Marcellus names Quintus Fulvius, who is himself elected consul with Fabius over the tribunes' objection, which the senate overrules on grounds of wartime need. Embassies go to Syphax and to Ptolemy and Cleopatra, prodigies are expiated, and Laelius reports the capture of New Carthage. Provinces and legions are distributed, the survivors of Fulvius' army being sent to Sicily under the same disgrace as the troops of Cannae.","ideas":["Intelligence that Hasdrubal is to march into Italy alarms the senate","Dispute whether a man presiding at an election may be elected","Precedents (Trasimenus decree, Posthumius, Fabius) justify repeated consulships","Livy defends dating the fall of New Carthage to this year"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"An emergency magistrate, here appointed only to hold the elections, who must be named on Roman soil, meaning Italy."},{"t":"interrex","d":"A temporary holder of supreme power between consulships, cited as a precedent for self-election."}]},{"i":478,"j":481,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 8–11","title":"Reformed flamen, refusing colonies, and stern censors","summary":"Mamilius becomes the first plebeian chief curio, and the dissolute Caius Valerius Flaccus, forced to become flamen of Jupiter, reforms himself and wins back the flamen's seat in the senate. Valerius Laevinus organizes Sicily so well that its corn feeds Rome and the army. Twelve of the thirty Latin colonies, worn out by ten years of levies, tell the consuls they can give neither men nor money, causing panic in the senate; but eighteen colonies, speaking through Marcus Sextilius of Fregellae, pledge full support and are publicly honoured, while the twelve are punished by silence. The reserve gold is drawn from the treasury, prodigies are expiated, and the censors name Fabius chief of the senate and punish the cavalry of Cannae.","ideas":["A forced priesthood turns a bad youth into a respected man","The colonies' complaint: Romans sent abroad are lost as surely as captives","Livy names the eighteen loyal colonies on whom the empire then rested","Sempronius picks Fabius as first of the senate, the first man even in Hannibal's judgment"],"terms":[{"t":"flamen of Jupiter (flamen Dialis)","d":"Jupiter's special priest, whose ancient right to a senate seat had lapsed through the unworthiness of earlier holders."},{"t":"vicesimary gold","d":"Gold kept in the most sacred part of the treasury for extreme emergencies; 4,000 pounds were now drawn out."},{"t":"princeps senatus","d":"The chief of the senate, chosen by the censors and first to be asked his opinion."}]},{"i":482,"j":486,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 12–16","title":"Marcellus at Canusium and Fabius takes Tarentum","summary":"While Fabius moves on Tarentum, Marcellus forces Hannibal to fight near Canusium; after a drawn first day the Romans break and flee on the second. Marcellus rebukes his men so harshly that they beg to fight again, and next day the elephants, driven back by the javelins of a maniple led by Decimus Flavius, trample their own side and Hannibal loses 8,000 men. Fabius then wins Tarentum by treachery: the Bruttian commander of the garrison, in love with a soldier's sister, is induced to betray his section of wall while diversions draw off the defenders. The city is sacked, but Fabius leaves the Tarentines their angry gods; Hannibal, arriving too late, says the Romans too have their Hannibal, and his ambush planned through forged Metapontine letters is foiled by bad omens.","ideas":["Hannibal: this enemy can bear neither good nor bad fortune","Shame, barley rations and front-line duty for cohorts that lost standards","Panicked elephants do more damage to their own army than to the enemy","Tarentum lost and retaken by the same arts; Fabius spares its statues"],"terms":[{"t":"maniple","d":"A tactical company of the legion, here of the hastati, which turned the elephants with massed javelins."},{"t":"auspices","d":"Divination from birds before an undertaking; their refusal kept Fabius from Hannibal's ambush at Metapontum."}]},{"i":487,"j":490,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 17–20","title":"Scipio wins at Baecula and frees Massiva","summary":"Spanish chiefs, Edesco, Indibilis and Mandonius, come over to Scipio, Indibilis justifying his change of side by Carthaginian injustice; Scipio returns their families and strengthens his army with marines. At Baecula he storms Hasdrubal's two-tiered hill position, sending Laelius round one flank and himself round the other, and kills about 8,000; Hasdrubal escapes with his treasure and elephants toward the Pyrenees. Scipio frees the Spanish captives, refuses the title of king, and sends Masinissa's young nephew Massiva home with gifts. The Carthaginian generals resolve that Hasdrubal must march to Italy with Spanish troops, while at Rome Marcellus, accused by the tribune Publicius Bibulus, comes home to defend himself.","ideas":["Deserters are hated only when the cause, not the name, deserves it","Scipio prefers the title of general and rejects that of king","The release of Massiva earns the goodwill of Masinissa","Carthage decides to remove Spaniards from Scipio's influence by marching to Italy"],"terms":[{"t":"imperator","d":"The title of victorious general, conferred by soldiers, which Scipio says he values above the name of king."},{"t":"quaestor","d":"The army's financial officer, who sold the African prisoners and found Massiva among them."}]},{"i":491,"j":496,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 21–26","title":"Marcellus vindicated, then trapped near Venusia","summary":"Marcellus answers Bibulus' attack on him and the nobility so well that he is elected consul with Crispinus. Commands are assigned for twenty-one legions, prodigies delay the consuls, the Apollinarian games are fixed to a set day, and Arretium is secured with hostages and new locks. The senate defers judgment on the Tarentines and on Marcus Livius, whom Fabius says would not have needed to recover Tarentum if he had not lost it. Marcellus must build a separate temple to Virtue because the pontiffs forbid one shrine for two gods; the consuls then camp near Hannibal between Venusia and Bantia. Hannibal ambushes a Roman force at Petelia, and the two consuls ride out with 220 horsemen to inspect a wooded hill where Numidians lie hidden, despite reports of bad entrails.","ideas":["Popular charges against Marcellus collapse before his record","Religious rule: one temple cannot be dedicated to Honour and Virtue together","Fabius' barb about Livius and the lost citadel","Hannibal, unequal to two consuls, turns to ambush"],"terms":[{"t":"Ludi Apollinares","d":"Games for Apollo, now vowed in perpetuity for a fixed day because of the pestilence."},{"t":"haruspex (aruspex)","d":"A diviner who reads the entrails of victims, here unsettled by a liver without a head."}]},{"i":497,"j":499,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 27–29","title":"Death of Marcellus and Hannibal's failed ruses","summary":"The Numidians spring from the hill; the Etruscan horsemen flee, the Fregellans fight on, Marcellus is killed by a lance, and Crispinus and Marcellus' son escape wounded. Livy blames the over-sixty general's rashness and notes Caelius' three versions of the event. Hannibal buries Marcellus and uses his signet ring to forge a letter to Salapia, but Crispinus has warned the towns; the Salapians drop the portcullis on about six hundred Roman deserters and kill them. Hannibal then relieves Locri, routing Cincius' besiegers. Crispinus, gravely wounded, asks the senate to send advisers; Valerius raids Africa and beats a Carthaginian fleet near Clupea; Philip moves to aid the Achaeans against Machanidas and the Aetolians.","ideas":["Marcellus dies through eagerness unbecoming a veteran","Livy weighs conflicting sources on the death","Hannibal caught by his own stratagem at Salapia","War spreads to Africa's coast and Greece"],"terms":[{"t":"lictors","d":"Attendants carrying the fasces before a consul; five of Marcellus' were captured alive."}]},{"i":500,"j":503,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 30–33","title":"Philip's campaign in Greece; Crispinus dies","summary":"Philip beats the Aetolians twice near Lamia; envoys from Egypt, Rhodes, Athens and Chios seek peace for fear of Macedonian power in Greece, but the Aetolians wreck the talks at Aegium with new demands once Attalus and a Roman fleet are near. Philip presides over the Nemean games, pretending to be a private man while preying on wives, and drives off Sulpicius' raiders near Sicyon. At Elis he is unhorsed and barely rescued from a Roman cohort, recovers face by capturing Pyrgus, and hurries home against a Dardanian invasion, while a broken helmet horn spreads a rumour of his death. Crispinus names Manlius Torquatus dictator and dies of his wound, and the senate resolves to choose consuls proof against Punic trickery.","ideas":["Mediators fear Philip more than they favour the Aetolians","Philip's show of equality covers his lust","Philip fights on foot at Elis and is carried from the field","Two consuls slain without a memorable battle leaves the state orphaned"],"terms":[{"t":"Nemean and Heraean games","d":"Argive festivals whose presidency Philip received because Macedonian kings traced their line to Argos."}]},{"i":504,"j":508,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 34–38","title":"Nero and Livius chosen as consuls","summary":"The senate picks Caius Claudius Nero and, to temper his vehemence, Marcus Livius, who had lived in sullen retirement since his condemnation by the people; Livius protests at the people's inconstancy but is persuaded with the example of Marcus Furius, recalled from exile to save his country. Fabius reconciles the two enemies, and they are given widely separated provinces, Bruttium against Hannibal and Gaul against Hasdrubal, whom Massilian reports place ready to cross the Alps in spring. The census shows 137,108 citizens, far fewer than before the war. Prodigies are expiated, including the drowning of an androgynous infant and a procession of twenty-seven maidens singing a hymn by Livius to Juno Regina. The consuls hold an unusually strict levy, cancel most coastal colonies' exemptions, and enlist volunteer slaves.","ideas":["Livius: if guilty, why trust me; if innocent, why condemn me?","Private enmity set aside at the senate's urging","The census reveals the war's losses","The maidens' procession and hymn to Juno Regina"],"terms":[{"t":"lustrum","d":"The purificatory sacrifice closing a census, performed this year for the first time since Hannibal's arrival."},{"t":"novendiale sacrum","d":"The nine days' rite performed after a shower of stones."}]},{"i":509,"j":513,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 39–43","title":"Hasdrubal crosses the Alps; Nero's secret march","summary":"Hasdrubal crosses the Alps faster than expected, helped by roads left from Hannibal's march and by Gallic and Alpine peoples who now know the Carthaginians are only passing through, but he wastes time besieging Placentia. Rome sees two Hannibals in Italy at once; Livius tells Fabius he will fight on sight, for glory or for joy at his countrymen's defeat. Hostilius mauls Hannibal's column, and Nero, with a picked army, beats him at Grumentum using cohorts hidden behind bare hills and follows him to Venusia and Canusium. Hasdrubal's riders carrying a letter to Hannibal are captured; learning of the meeting planned in Umbria, Nero tells the senate to guard Narnia and marches north by night with 6,000 foot and 1,000 horse to join his colleague.","ideas":["The Alps opened by goodwill and earlier roads","Rome's earlier recovery depended on distance between theatres, now lost","An ambush laid on naked hills where none was suspected","The intercepted letter prompts Nero to leave his province"],"terms":[]},{"i":514,"j":517,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 44–47","title":"Rome's fears and Nero joins Livius","summary":"Rome is as alarmed as when Hannibal stood at the gates, judging the march only by its outcome and dwelling on Hasdrubal's reputation. Nero tells his men the bold plan is in truth safe, since rumour of a second consul will decide the battle, and the people along the road feed them and pray for them. Livius hides the arrival by doubling up his men in the same tents; at the council Nero insists on fighting at once, before Hannibal discovers the trick. Hasdrubal notices worn shields, lean horses and greater numbers, and hears the signal sounded twice in the consul's camp, so he concludes that both consuls are present and retreats by night. Deserted by his guides, he loses his way along the winding Metaurus.","ideas":["Judging a plan only by success is called unfair","Nero: the last force added gets the credit for the whole","One trumpet signal or two betrays the presence of a second consul","Lost guides and high riverbanks trap Hasdrubal"],"terms":[{"t":"trumpet signal","d":"The trumpet call in camp, sounded once for a praetor and twice for a consul."}]},{"i":518,"j":522,"loc":"Book XXVII, chapters 48–51","title":"Battle of the Metaurus and death of Hasdrubal","summary":"Overtaken by Nero's cavalry, Porcius and Livius, Hasdrubal puts his elephants in front, the Ligurians in the centre, the Spaniards on his right against Livius and the Gauls on his left, screened by a hill. The fiercest fighting is between Livius and the Spaniards; Nero, blocked by the hill, leads cohorts behind the line and falls on the enemy flank and rear. More elephants are killed by their own riders, who drive a knife into the neck, than by the enemy, and Hasdrubal, after rallying his men, rides into a Roman cohort and dies. Livy reports 56,000 enemy dead, reckoned a return for Cannae; Livius lets the fugitives go to carry the news. Nero is back in his camp in six days, Rome passes from suspense to rejoicing and a three-day supplication, and Hasdrubal's head is thrown before Hannibal's outposts; Hannibal sees the fate of Carthage and withdraws into Bruttium.","ideas":["Nero's flank march behind the line decides the battle","Hasdrubal dies worthy of Hamilcar's son and Hannibal's brother","Rome's slow belief in the news and the revival of business","Hannibal recognises Carthage's destiny in his brother's head"],"terms":[{"t":"supplicatio","d":"Public thanksgiving at all the temples, decreed for three days after the victory."}]},{"i":523,"j":530,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 1–4","title":"Silanus in Celtiberia and the capture of Orinx","summary":"After an epitome of the book, Livy turns to Spain, where a new war has arisen after Hasdrubal's departure for Italy. Silanus, guided by deserters, surprises the camps of Hanno and Mago in Celtiberia; the raw Celtiberians are cut down in broken ground, Hanno is captured and Mago flees to Hasdrubal son of Gisgo at Gades. Hasdrubal scatters his troops among the towns, so Scipio sends his brother Lucius to storm Orinx, where fleeing townsmen are mistakenly killed but the rest are spared. Meanwhile Laevinus ravages the country round Utica and Carthage and defeats a Carthaginian fleet.","ideas":["Surprise attack on the poorly guarded Celtiberian camp","Hasdrubal disperses his army into fortified towns","Lucius Scipio storms Orinx; Scipio ranks it with New Carthage","Roman raids in Africa and a naval victory bring corn to Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"Celtiberia","d":"The inland region of Spain between the two seas, where Hanno and Mago raised new troops."}]},{"i":531,"j":534,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 5–8","title":"Philip of Macedon against Sulpicius and Attalus","summary":"In Greece the Roman proconsul Sulpicius and King Attalus join fleets while Philip is beset by appeals from allies threatened by the Aetolians and by Machanidas of Sparta. Oreus falls to the Romans through the treachery of its commander Plator, but Chalcis, guarded by the violent Euripus and loyal magistrates, is given up. Philip rushes through Thermopylae and nearly catches Attalus plundering Opus; Attalus then leaves for Asia because Prusias has invaded his kingdom. Philip, always arriving too late, boasts that the enemy flee from him, restores towns to the Achaeans, raids Aetolia, and returns home to fight the Dardanians.","ideas":["Signal fires and watch-towers as Philip's warning system","Treachery wins Oreus; the Euripus defeats the attempt on Chalcis","Philip's speed always just misses its object","Attalus withdraws when Prusias attacks his kingdom"],"terms":[{"t":"Euripus","d":"The narrow strait at Chalcis, whose irregular currents Livy describes as dangerous to ships."}]},{"i":535,"j":539,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 9–11","title":"Triumph of Livius and Nero; the new year","summary":"The consuls Livius and Claudius Nero return together and share a triumph for the victory over Hasdrubal: Livius rides in a chariot with his army, Nero on horseback without troops, yet the crowd gives Nero the greater praise for his march. Veturius and Caecilius are elected consuls under Livius as dictator, and provinces and armies are allotted for the thirteenth year of the war. Prodigies are expiated, and the going out of the Vestal fire leads to the scourging of the Vestal on watch. The senate orders the people back to farming and the colonists back to Placentia and Cremona; the consuls raid Consentia and recover Lucania.","ideas":["A joint triumph in which the lesser display earns the greater glory","Allotment of provinces and legions for the new year","Prodigies and the extinguished fire of Vesta","Resettling the devastated countryside of Italy"],"terms":[{"t":"triumph","d":"The victory procession to the Capitol decreed by the senate to a successful general."},{"t":"supplication","d":"A decreed day of public prayer to the gods, for thanks or for expiation of prodigies."}]},{"i":540,"j":544,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 12–16","title":"Hannibal in Bruttium; Scipio's victory at Silpia","summary":"Livy admires Hannibal for keeping a mixed army of many nations free of mutiny even when confined to Bruttium. In Spain Hasdrubal son of Gisgo and Mago gather a huge army near Silpia; Scipio collects allied auxiliaries but will not rely on them too heavily. After days of drawn-up armies that never engage, Scipio attacks at dawn with Romans on the wings and Spaniards in the centre, wears out the unfed enemy and routs them. Allies desert Hasdrubal, the fleeing army is cut down on the way to the ocean, and Hasdrubal escapes by sea to Gades; Masinissa secretly confers with Silanus, and the Carthaginians are driven out of Spain.","ideas":["Hannibal more admirable in adversity than in prosperity","Scipio reverses his battle order on the day of battle","Delaying the clash until the enemy is hungry and tired","The expulsion of the Carthaginians from Spain"],"terms":[{"t":"Silpia","d":"The city near which the Carthaginian generals gathered their army and were defeated (the battle often called Ilipa)."}]},{"i":545,"j":551,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 17–23","title":"Syphax, Illiturgi, Castulo and the fall of Astapa","summary":"Looking now to Africa, Scipio crosses with two ships to King Syphax, arriving the same day as Hasdrubal; the two dine on one couch, Hasdrubal is struck by Scipio's charm, and Scipio makes a league with the king. Back in Spain he punishes Illiturgi for murdering Roman fugitives: after a hard defence the town is stormed and its people slaughtered, while Castulo is surrendered and treated mildly. Scipio holds a gladiatorial show of volunteers for his father and uncle, where the princes Corbis and Orsua fight for a throne. Marcius attacks Astapa, whose citizens burn their families and goods and die fighting; deserters from Gades then offer to betray the town.","ideas":["Scipio and Hasdrubal as rival guests of Syphax","Vengeance on Illiturgi versus leniency to Castulo","Funeral games with volunteer combatants","The mass self-destruction of Astapa"],"terms":[{"t":"lanistae","d":"Trainers who supplied slave or hired gladiators, whom Scipio's show did not use."}]},{"i":552,"j":554,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 24–26","title":"The mutiny at Sucro and its suppression","summary":"A rumour that the sick Scipio has died sets Spain in ferment: Mandonius and Indibilis rise, and the eight thousand idle soldiers at Sucro mutiny over pay, drive out their tribunes and hand the fasces to the common soldiers Albius and Atrius. When Scipio is found alive the mutiny collapses, and tribunes sent by him calm the men with mild words and promises of pay. A council at Carthage decides to punish only the thirty-five ringleaders. The mutineers are summoned for pay, the ringleaders are seized at dinner, and the rest are surrounded by armed troops in the forum, stunned by the sight of a healthy and stern Scipio.","ideas":["Idleness and plunder-habits as causes of the mutiny","Common soldiers usurp the insignia of command","Punishment limited to the originators","A feigned march used to trap the mutineers"],"terms":[{"t":"fasces","d":"The rods and axes carried before a Roman magistrate as the sign of his authority."}]},{"i":555,"j":557,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 27–29","title":"Scipio's speech to the mutineers","summary":"Scipio asks what to call men who have acted like enemies, compares a crowd to a sea stirred by winds and blames the ringleaders for the contagion. He sets their crime beside the legion at Rhegium and Coriolanus, and argues that Rome would not have died with him, since it has outlived many fallen generals. He promises to forget the offence of the many if they repent, but declares that Albius, Atrius and the other leaders must pay with their blood. The ringleaders are then scourged and beheaded before the silent assembly, and the rest swear allegiance and receive their pay.","ideas":["The multitude likened to the sea moved by storms","The state does not perish with one general","Pardon for the many, death for the leaders","Discipline restored through public execution"],"terms":[{"t":"sacramentum (military oath)","d":"The soldier's oath to his general, renewed here by each man named."}]},{"i":558,"j":562,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 30–34","title":"Carteia, and the defeat of Indibilis and Mandonius","summary":"Marcius scatters Hanno's new levies on the Baetis, and Laelius fights a chaotic sea-fight in the strait off Carteia, where the currents rule the ships; but the plot to betray Gades has been discovered. Hearing of the punishment of the mutineers, Indibilis and Mandonius take up arms again. Scipio tells his army he grieved to punish his countrymen but goes gladly against these robbers, then traps the Spaniards in an enclosed valley with cattle as bait and a hidden cavalry move, destroying those engaged. Mandonius begs for mercy, and Scipio spares the chiefs, leaving them their arms and hostages and imposing only a fine.","ideas":["A naval battle decided by current rather than skill","Rebel chiefs resume war on hearing of the executions","Two separate battles of horse and foot in a narrow valley","Clemency instead of the usual full surrender"],"terms":[{"t":"deditio (surrender)","d":"The ancient Roman custom of requiring a full surrender of all things, sacred and profane, before ruling a people."}]},{"i":563,"j":566,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 35–38","title":"Masinissa, Mago's departure, Scipio elected consul","summary":"Masinissa slips away from Gades to meet Scipio, is awed by his appearance, and pledges to serve Rome, promising Carthage would soon fall if Scipio came to Africa. Mago, ordered to take his fleet to Italy, plunders Gades, fails in a night attack on New Carthage, crucifies the Gaditane magistrates, and winters on the lesser Balearic island after the slingers of the larger repel him; Gades then surrenders to Rome. Scipio reports to the senate that he left no Carthaginian in Spain, does not press for a triumph, and is elected consul with Licinius Crassus amid huge crowds who expect him to end the war in Africa.","ideas":["Masinissa's personal compact with Scipio","Mago leaves Spain for Italy via the Balearic islands","No triumph for a commander without a magistracy","Scipio's election and popular hopes for Africa"],"terms":[{"t":"suffetes","d":"The chief magistrates of Carthaginian cities, as at Gades."}]},{"i":567,"j":571,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 38–42","title":"The Saguntines' thanks and Fabius against Africa","summary":"Scipio receives Sicily as his province and the Saguntine envoys thank the senate for their restoration and are allowed to place a golden crown on the Capitol. When Scipio is said to want Africa and threatens to go to the people, Quintus Fabius Maximus argues against the invasion. He denies envy, urges that Hannibal be driven from Italy first, and warns of the cost of two armies, the danger to Rome, and the fates of Regulus and the Athenians in Sicily. He says Numidian kings and Spanish allies are untrustworthy, and accuses Scipio of seeking personal glory over the state.","ideas":["Saguntum as a monument of faith kept on both sides","Peace in Italy before war in Africa","Warnings from Regulus and the Athenian expedition to Sicily","Armies serve the state, not consuls' ambitions"],"terms":[{"t":"provincia","d":"The sphere of command assigned to a magistrate, here the disputed question whether Africa should be one."}]},{"i":572,"j":574,"loc":"Book XXVIII, chs. 43–45","title":"Scipio's reply and the senate's compromise","summary":"Scipio answers that Fabius's suspicion of jealousy is not removed, recalls that no one feared for him when he took Spain at twenty-four, and counters Regulus with Agathocles and Hannibal himself. He argues that carrying war to the enemy shakes faithless Carthaginian allies and draws Hannibal after him, and that Licinius can hold Italy. Quintus Fulvius demands whether Scipio will abide by the senate, and the tribunes force the question back to the senate. Sicily is decreed to Scipio with leave to cross to Africa; without a levy he takes volunteers, and Etruscan and Italian communities supply materials for a fleet built in forty-five days.","ideas":["Ambition for glory defended as serving the state","Agathocles as precedent for invading Africa","Tribunes compel Scipio to submit to the senate","Allied contributions build Scipio's fleet"],"terms":[]},{"i":575,"j":585,"loc":"Book XXVIII 46 – Book XXIX 3","title":"Scipio prepares in Sicily; Spanish revolt crushed","summary":"Scipio sails to Sicily with volunteers while Mago lands in Liguria, takes Genoa and gathers Gauls, alarming the senate; Hannibal winters by the temple of Juno Lacinia. Book XXIX opens with Scipio arming three hundred Roman youths at the expense of reluctant Sicilian horsemen, choosing Marcellus' veterans, and restoring Syracusan property. In Spain Indibilis raises the Ilergetians and Ausetanians, but Lentulus and Manlius defeat them; Indibilis dies fighting and Mandonius is handed over. Laelius raids Hippo Regius, and Carthage, believing Scipio has landed, despairs at its weakened position.","ideas":["Scipio replaces unwilling Sicilian cavalry with Roman youths at no cost","Indibilis' rising ends with his death and the surrender of Mandonius","Mago's landing in Liguria renews fear of a war in northern Italy","Laelius' raid shows Carthage how exposed Africa is"],"terms":[{"t":"Ilergetians","d":"The Spanish people of Indibilis and Mandonius, leaders of the revolt against Rome."},{"t":"Juno Lacinia","d":"The temple near Croton where Hannibal set up an altar recording his deeds in Punic and Greek."}]},{"i":586,"j":591,"loc":"Book XXIX 4–9","title":"Carthage arms; Locri retaken; Pleminius' crimes","summary":"Carthage levies troops, courts Syphax and Philip, and reinforces Mago, while Masinissa urges Laelius to make Scipio cross quickly. Mago consults the Gauls and Ligurians, who promise covert or delayed help. Exiled Locrian nobles use ransomed artisans to betray a citadel of Locri to Roman troops; Hannibal arrives, but Scipio sails from Messana, sallies, and forces him to withdraw. Left in command, Pleminius and his soldiers outdo the Carthaginians in rapacity, rob the treasury of Proserpine, and fall into a bloody feud with the tribunes; Scipio acquits Pleminius, who then tortures the tribunes to death.","ideas":["Masinissa presses for an early invasion of Africa","Locri is recovered through betrayal of its citadel","Plunder of Proserpine's treasure is followed by madness among the plunderers","Scipio's lenient judgment lets Pleminius' cruelty grow"],"terms":[{"t":"Proserpine's treasury","d":"The sacred treasure at Locri, once taken and restored by Pyrrhus, which Pleminius' men plunder."},{"t":"propraetor / lieutenant-general","d":"Pleminius' rank as Scipio's deputy commanding the garrison at Locri."}]},{"i":592,"j":598,"loc":"Book XXIX 10–16","title":"The Idaean Mother comes to Rome","summary":"A Sibylline prophecy says a foreign invader can be driven from Italy if the Idaean Mother is brought from Pessinus; envoys, guided by Delphi, obtain the sacred stone through king Attalus. Philip makes peace with the Aetolians and then, at Phoenice, with Rome. Provinces and commands are assigned, and the senate judges the young Publius Scipio Nasica the best man in Rome; he receives the goddess at Ostia, and the matrons, among them Claudia Quinta, carry her to the Palatine. The twelve defaulting Latin colonies are compelled to furnish double levies, and wartime loans are ordered repaid.","ideas":["Prophecy, oracle and Scipio's hopes are read as pointing to victory","Scipio Nasica is chosen as best of the good men to receive the goddess","The Peace of Phoenice ends the war with Philip for now","The twelve Latin colonies are punished for refusing soldiers"],"terms":[{"t":"Idaean Mother","d":"The mother of the gods, worshipped at Pessinus in Phrygia as a sacred stone, brought to Rome."},{"t":"Megalesian games","d":"The games instituted on the arrival of the Idaean Mother."},{"t":"Sibylline books","d":"The prophetic books consulted by the decemviri in times of portents."}]},{"i":599,"j":604,"loc":"Book XXIX 16–21","title":"The Locrian complaint and Scipio's vindication","summary":"The eldest Locrian envoy describes Pleminius as a monster who has made every soldier a plunderer, and recounts how Proserpine has always punished violators of her temple. Fabius attacks Scipio as a corrupter of discipline who lounges in Greek dress, and proposes his recall; Metellus carries a milder motion sending the praetor Pomponius with a commission to investigate. The commission restores the sacred money and the Locrians' liberty, and the Locrians clear Scipio of having ordered the crimes. Pleminius is sent in chains to Rome and dies in prison, while Scipio's display of his army and fleet at Syracuse convinces the commissioners, and the senate authorizes him to cross to Africa.","ideas":["The Locrians compare Roman rule unfavourably with Carthaginian","Fabius' hostility to Scipio is checked by Metellus","The goddess's treasure is restored and expiated","Scipio answers charges of luxury by showing his preparations"],"terms":[{"t":"conscript fathers","d":"The customary form of address to the Roman senate."},{"t":"college of pontiffs","d":"The priestly body consulted on the expiation of the sacrilege at Locri."}]},{"i":605,"j":610,"loc":"Book XXIX 22–27","title":"Syphax defects; Scipio crosses to Africa","summary":"Hasdrubal son of Gisgo marries his daughter to Syphax, binds him to Carthage by treaty, and has him warn Scipio not to invade. Scipio hides the message, tells his army the kings are urging haste, and gathers his forces at Lilybaeum, taking the legions of Cannae. Livy notes the disputed numbers, describes the orderly embarkation and the vast crowd at the departure, and gives Scipio's prayer before sailing. After mist and a night at anchor the fleet lands at the Fair Promontory.","ideas":["Syphax's marriage to Hasdrubal's daughter turns him against Rome","Scipio conceals the bad news with a false account","The Cannae legions hope to redeem their disgrace","Scipio takes the cape of Pulcher as a good omen for landing"],"terms":[{"t":"Lilybaeum","d":"The Sicilian port from which Scipio's fleet sailed for Africa."},{"t":"Cape of Pulcher (Fair Promontory)","d":"The landing place whose name Scipio welcomed as an omen."}]},{"i":611,"j":616,"loc":"Book XXIX 28–33","title":"Masinissa joins Scipio: his fortunes recounted","summary":"The landing throws Carthage into a panic it has not known since Regulus. Scipio's cavalry kills the commander Hanno, a rich town is taken, and Masinissa arrives, prompting Livy's digression on his life. After his father Gala's death the Massylian throne passes to Oesalces and Capusa, whom Mezetulus overthrows; Masinissa returns from Spain, wins back his kingdom and reconciles his rivals. Urged by Hasdrubal, Syphax drives him out; Masinissa lives as a raider on Mount Balbus, escapes Bocchar by swimming a river while thought dead, and recovers his realm, only to be routed again by Syphax and his son Vermina. He waits near the Lesser Syrtis until Laelius comes, which leads Livy to judge that he joined Scipio with a small force.","ideas":["Numidian succession passes to brothers before sons","Masinissa survives repeated defeats and is reported dead","Hasdrubal persuades Syphax that Masinissa is a rising danger","Livy weighs the conflicting figures for Masinissa's cavalry"],"terms":[{"t":"Massylians","d":"Masinissa's hereditary Numidian people."},{"t":"Masaesylians","d":"The Numidian kingdom of Syphax."}]},{"i":617,"j":622,"loc":"Book XXIX 34–38","title":"Salera, the siege of Utica, and the censors","summary":"Masinissa feigns flight and draws Hanno's cavalry from Salera into a Roman ambush, where Hanno and many horsemen are killed. Scipio ravages the land and besieges Utica for forty days, until Hasdrubal and Syphax arrive with great armies and he fortifies a winter camp on a promontory. In Italy the consul Sempronius, after a reverse near Croton, joins Licinius and defeats Hannibal, and Cornelius holds Etruria by trials. At Rome the censors Livius and Claudius Nero institute the salt tax and degrade each other, Livius disfranchising thirty-four tribes; the book closes with elections and priesthoods.","ideas":["Masinissa's feigned retreat destroys Hanno's cavalry","Scipio abandons the siege of Utica for winter quarters","Hannibal is beaten near Croton","The censors' quarrel and Livius' surname Salinator"],"terms":[{"t":"Salinator","d":"Surname given to Marcus Livius from the salt tax he imposed as censor."},{"t":"lustrum","d":"The ceremonial closing of the census by the censors."},{"t":"Fortuna Primigenia","d":"The goddess to whom Sempronius vowed a temple before his victory."}]},{"i":623,"j":633,"loc":"Book XXX 1–7","title":"Scipio burns the camps of Syphax and Hasdrubal","summary":"Book XXX opens with the provinces for the sixteenth year, Scipio's command extended until the war ends, the naval guards, and prodigies. During the winter Scipio keeps up peace talks with Syphax, sending centurions disguised as servants to study the enemy's wooden and reed huts. He breaks off the truce, marches by night, and has Laelius and Masinissa fire Syphax's camp, then attacks Hasdrubal's as men rush out unarmed to fight the flames; some forty thousand die. At Carthage the Barcine faction carries a vote to fight on, and Syphax, moved by Sophonisba's pleas and news of Celtiberian recruits, joins Hasdrubal again.","ideas":["Peace talks serve as cover for spying on the enemy camps","Scipio ends the truce first so as not to break faith","The night fire destroys two armies","Sophonisba keeps Syphax in the war"],"terms":[{"t":"suffetes","d":"The chief magistrates of Carthage, compared by Livy to consuls."},{"t":"Barcine faction","d":"Hannibal's family party at Carthage, which favoured continuing the war."}]},{"i":634,"j":638,"loc":"Book XXX 8–12","title":"Great Plains, Tunes, and the fall of Syphax","summary":"Scipio defeats Syphax and Hasdrubal on the Great Plains, where the Celtiberians die fighting to the last, and seizes Tunes in sight of Carthage. The Carthaginian fleet attacks his siege ships at Utica, but he ties his transports into a floating wall; the enemy tows away only about six. Laelius and Masinissa invade Numidia, rout Syphax's raw army, and capture the king when his horse falls. Masinissa shows Syphax in chains to Cirta to make it surrender, meets Sophonisba at the palace, promises she will not fall to the Romans, and marries her that day, to Laelius' displeasure.","ideas":["The Celtiberians' stand covers the flight of the generals","Scipio's chained transports serve as a wall against the fleet","Syphax is captured and Cirta opened","Masinissa marries Sophonisba to keep her from Roman hands"],"terms":[{"t":"Great Plains","d":"The open ground near Utica where Scipio defeated the reunited armies."},{"t":"harpoons","d":"Hooked beams the Carthaginians threw to drag off Roman transports."}]},{"i":639,"j":643,"loc":"Book XXX 13–17","title":"Sophonisba's death and Carthage sues for peace","summary":"Syphax, brought before Scipio, blames his madness on his Carthaginian wife and says the same ruin now enters Masinissa's house. Scipio privately rebukes Masinissa, praising self-control over pleasure and claiming Sophonisba as Roman spoil. Masinissa sends her poison as his second promise, and she drinks it calmly; Scipio consoles him and hails him king with gifts. The Carthaginian elders prostrate themselves and accept Scipio's terms to gain time for Hannibal, while at Rome Laelius reports the victories and the senate confirms Masinissa's title and gifts.","ideas":["Syphax blames Sophonisba for his defection","Scipio holds temperance the greatest of his virtues","Sophonisba accepts the poison as a wedding gift","Carthage seeks a truce mainly to recall Hannibal"],"terms":[{"t":"curule chair and ivory sceptre","d":"Roman insignia Scipio grants Masinissa when naming him king."},{"t":"council of elders","d":"The Carthaginian body of thirty elders, said to control even the senate, sent to sue for peace."}]},{"i":644,"j":651,"loc":"Book XXX 18–25","title":"Mago and Hannibal leave Italy; truce broken","summary":"Quinctilius Varus and Cornelius defeat Mago among the Insubrians after his elephants are turned back; Mago, wounded, is recalled and dies at sea off Sardinia. Hannibal, recalled too, blames the Carthaginian senate and Hanno, kills Italians who refuse to follow him, and sails off cursing that he did not march on Rome after Cannae. At Rome the Carthaginian envoys blame Hannibal and ask for the old treaty but cannot recall its terms, and are dismissed. During the truce Carthage seizes Roman transports wrecked near the city and attacks Scipio's envoys at sea; Scipio prepares for war as Hannibal lands at Leptis.","ideas":["Mago's wound ends the Carthaginian war in the north","Hannibal claims he was beaten by envy at home, not by Rome","The young Carthaginian envoys cannot recall the old treaty","Carthage breaks the truce by seizing ships and attacking envoys"],"terms":[{"t":"truce","d":"The armistice granted while envoys went to Rome, broken by the seizure of the Roman convoy."},{"t":"Aegimurus","d":"Island at the mouth of the bay of Carthage where many Roman transports were driven."}]},{"i":652,"j":656,"loc":"Book XXX 26–30","title":"Fabius dies; Hannibal and Scipio meet","summary":"Envoys are sent to protest Philip's aid to Carthage, Rome suffers fire and flood, and Livy gives an appreciation of Quintus Fabius Maximus, the delayer who restored the state. The new consuls contend for Africa, but the people vote for Scipio. Both Rome and Carthage wait anxiously for the meeting of the two great generals. Hannibal marches to Zama, and Scipio shows his spies around the camp and sends them back; Hannibal asks for a meeting, and in a long speech urges Scipio to prefer a certain peace to an uncertain victory, offering Sicily, Sardinia, Spain and the islands.","ideas":["Fabius is praised as the man whose delay saved Rome","Fear grows that Hannibal will be more dangerous at home","Scipio sends Hannibal's spies back after a full tour","Hannibal warns that fortune is least reliable at its height"],"terms":[{"t":"the one man who restored the state by delay","d":"The character Livy gives Fabius, citing Ennius' line that one man restored the state by delay."},{"t":"Zama","d":"The town, five days from Carthage, where Hannibal concentrated before the final battle."}]},{"i":657,"j":661,"loc":"Book XXX 31–35","title":"Scipio rejects terms; the battle of Zama","summary":"Scipio replies that Carthage broke the truce and that Hannibal offers only what Rome already holds; without compensation, there must be war. Both generals tell their armies the world is the prize. Scipio leaves lanes between his maniples for the elephants, with Laelius and Masinissa on the wings; Hannibal sets eighty elephants in front, then mercenaries, then Carthaginians and Africans, with the Italians in reserve. The elephants turn back, the cavalry are routed, the first lines collapse and fight each other, and Laelius and Masinissa return to strike the rear. About twenty thousand Carthaginians are killed; Hannibal escapes to Adrumetum and tells the senate that only peace remains.","ideas":["Scipio demands compensation for the broken truce","Lanes in the Roman line neutralize the elephants","The Carthaginian lines turn on one another","Even Scipio praises Hannibal's dispositions"],"terms":[{"t":"hastati, principes, triarii","d":"The three lines of the Roman legion, which Scipio redeploys during the battle."},{"t":"velites","d":"Roman light-armed troops placed in the lanes to harass the elephants."}]},{"i":662,"j":666,"loc":"Book XXX 36–40","title":"Terms imposed on Carthage after Zama","summary":"Scipio sails to Carthage to frighten it, sends the suppliant envoys to Tunes, and destroys Vermina's Numidian army. He offers peace terms, partly because a siege would be long and a successor might take the glory: Carthage keeps its laws and territory but gives up its warships and elephants, may make no war without Rome's leave, restores Masinissa, and pays ten thousand talents over fifty years. When Gisgo speaks against the terms, Hannibal pulls him down and argues for peace. A truce is granted, Claudius' fleet is wrecked by storms, and at Rome the victory is celebrated while the new consul Lentulus tries to claim Africa.","ideas":["Scipio's terms leave Carthage free but disarmed and dependent","Hannibal drags Gisgo from the platform and urges acceptance","Storms keep the consul Claudius from reaching Africa","The tribunes resist Lentulus' bid for Scipio's command"],"terms":[{"t":"hostages","d":"One hundred Carthaginians aged 14 to 30, chosen by Scipio as security for the peace."},{"t":"Saturnalia","d":"The festival on whose first day Vermina's army was routed."}]},{"i":667,"j":672,"loc":"Book XXX 41–45","title":"Peace ratified; Scipio's triumph as Africanus","summary":"Provinces are assigned, and Philip's envoys are told the king is seeking war. Hasdrubal Haedus pleads for Carthage, saying Rome grew more by sparing the conquered, and the people vote that Scipio should grant the peace and bring the army home. Carthage hands over its warships, elephants, deserters and four thousand prisoners, and Scipio has the ships burnt. Hannibal, seen laughing amid the grief over the tribute, says they should have wept when their arms were taken. Masinissa receives Cirta, and Scipio returns through rejoicing Italy to a splendid triumph and the surname Africanus.","ideas":["Rome warns Philip, foreshadowing a new war","The burning of the Carthaginian fleet","Hannibal's bitter laughter over the indemnity","Scipio is the first general named for a conquered nation"],"terms":[{"t":"heralds' flint and vervain","d":"The sacred stones and herbs the heralds took from Rome to strike the treaty."},{"t":"Africanus","d":"Scipio's surname, the first taken from a conquered people; Livy is unsure who gave it."}]}]});